/* Community Medicine 3–4: Biostatistics; communicable diseases & programmes */
Z("cm3",0,"cm",["Measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode) summarise the 'average' value of data, while measures of dispersion (range, mean deviation, standard deviation, variance, coefficient of variation) describe how spread out the values are.","Central tendency tells where the centre of the crowd stands; dispersion tells how scattered the crowd is around it.",["Mean = sum of values / number (affected by extreme values); median = middle value of ordered data (best for skewed data, e.g., income, incubation period); mode = most frequent value (can be bimodal).","Range = highest − lowest (simple, crude); interquartile range (Q3 − Q1; with median); mean deviation; standard deviation (SD) = √[Σ(x − x̄)²/(n − 1)] — most important; variance = SD².","Coefficient of variation = SD/mean × 100 — compares variability of different units (height vs weight).","Normal distribution: mean = median = mode; skewed data: positive skew — mean > median > mode; negative skew — mean < median < mode."],"Skewed data → report median; normal data → mean ± SD."],
"Biostatistics summarises data by measures of central tendency (location) and dispersion (variability). Measures of central tendency — the 'average' value around which data cluster: (1) arithmetic mean — sum of all observations divided by the number of observations (x̄ = Σx/n); advantages — easy to calculate and understand, uses all observations, amenable to further statistical treatment; disadvantages — greatly affected by extreme values (outliers), may not correspond to an actual value; weighted mean, geometric mean (for log-normal data such as antibody titres, bacterial counts), harmonic mean; (2) median — the middle value when observations are arranged in ascending or descending order; for odd n, the ((n+1)/2)th value; for even n, the average of the two middle values; not affected by extreme values — preferred for skewed distributions (income, hospital stay, incubation period, survival time); does not use all observations; percentiles, quartiles (Q1 25th, Q2 = median, Q3 75th); (3) mode — the most frequently occurring value; can be more than one (bimodal — e.g., age distribution of Hodgkin lymphoma); useful for qualitative data; in a frequency distribution, mode can be estimated as 3 median − 2 mean (empirical relationship). In a symmetrical (normal) distribution, mean = median = mode; in positively (right) skewed distributions mean > median > mode; in negatively (left) skewed distributions mean < median < mode. Measures of dispersion — the degree of spread or variation of observations around the central value; two sets of data may have the same mean but different variability: (1) range — difference between the highest and lowest values; simplest but crude, depends only on extreme values, affected by sample size; (2) interquartile range — Q3 − Q1 (middle 50% of data); used with median for skewed data; semi-interquartile range; (3) mean deviation — average of absolute deviations of observations from the mean (or median); (4) standard deviation (root-mean-square deviation) — the most frequently used measure; SD = √[Σ(x − x̄)²/(n − 1)] (n − 1 for sample — Bessel's correction); greater the SD, greater the dispersion; properties — uses all observations, mathematically tractable, basis for normal distribution, confidence intervals and tests of significance; in a normal distribution, mean ± 1 SD covers 68.3%, ± 2 SD 95.4% (± 1.96 SD — 95%), ± 3 SD 99.7% of observations; (5) variance — square of SD (σ²); (6) coefficient of variation — relative measure of dispersion: CV = (SD/mean) × 100; used to compare variability of two or more series with different units or widely different means (e.g., variability of height in cm vs weight in kg; reproducibility of laboratory tests); (7) standard error of mean — SD/√n — measures sampling variability of the mean (used for confidence intervals: mean ± 1.96 SE). Choice: for symmetrical quantitative data — mean ± SD; for skewed data or ordinal data — median and IQR; for categorical data — mode, proportions.",
[["Measures of central tendency",[["Measure","Definition","Advantage","Disadvantage"],["Mean","Σx/n","Uses all data, algebraic","Affected by extremes"],["Median","Middle value","Not affected by extremes; skewed data","Ignores magnitudes"],["Mode","Most frequent","Qualitative data; quick","May be multiple/absent"]]],
["Measures of dispersion",[["Measure","Formula / note"],["Range","Max − min"],["Interquartile range","Q3 − Q1"],["Mean deviation","Σ|x − x̄|/n"],["Standard deviation","√[Σ(x − x̄)²/(n − 1)]"],["Variance","SD²"],["Coefficient of variation","SD/mean × 100"],["Standard error","SD/√n"]]],
["Skewness",[["Distribution","Relationship"],["Symmetrical (normal)","Mean = median = mode"],["Positive (right) skew","Mean > median > mode"],["Negative (left) skew","Mean < median < mode"]]]],
{c:"Mean, median and mode describe the centre of data, while range, SD, variance and coefficient of variation describe its spread; median with IQR suits skewed data and mean with SD suits normally distributed data."});

Z("cm3",1,"cm",["The normal (Gaussian) distribution is a symmetrical, bell-shaped curve where mean, median and mode coincide; 68%, 95% and 99.7% of values lie within ±1, ±2 and ±3 standard deviations of the mean, forming the basis of reference ranges and tests of significance.","Most biological measurements (height, BP, Hb) pile up around the average and thin out evenly on both sides — the bell curve.",["Characteristics: bell-shaped, symmetrical about mean, mean = median = mode, asymptotic (tails never touch x-axis), total area = 1 (100%), defined by mean (μ) and SD (σ).","Areas: μ ± 1σ = 68.27%; μ ± 2σ = 95.45% (μ ± 1.96σ = 95%); μ ± 3σ = 99.73%.","Standard normal curve: mean 0, SD 1; z-score (standard normal deviate) = (x − μ)/σ — position of an observation relative to mean.","Uses: reference/normal ranges (mean ± 2 SD), growth charts, confidence intervals, z-test and parametric tests, quality control; non-normal data — transform or use non-parametric tests."],"68-95-99.7 rule for ±1, 2, 3 SD."],
"The normal distribution (Gaussian distribution, after Karl Friedrich Gauss) is the most important continuous probability distribution in statistics. When a large number of observations of a biological variable (height, weight, blood pressure, haemoglobin, serum cholesterol) are made on a homogeneous population and plotted as a frequency polygon with small class intervals, the curve approaches a smooth, symmetrical bell shape — the normal curve. Characteristics: (1) bell-shaped and symmetrical about the mean; (2) mean, median and mode coincide; (3) the curve is unimodal; (4) it is asymptotic — the tails extend indefinitely without touching the horizontal axis; (5) the total area under the curve is 1 (100% of observations); (6) the curve is completely defined by two parameters — mean (μ, location) and standard deviation (σ, spread); different means shift the curve, larger SD flattens it; (7) the points of inflection are at μ ± 1σ; (8) skewness = 0, kurtosis = 3 (mesokurtic). Area relationships: μ ± 1σ includes 68.27% of observations; μ ± 2σ — 95.45%; μ ± 3σ — 99.73%; μ ± 1.96σ — 95%; μ ± 2.58σ — 99%. Standard normal curve: a normal distribution with mean 0 and SD 1; any normal variable can be converted to the standard form by calculating the standard normal deviate (z-score) = (x − μ)/σ, which indicates how many SDs an observation lies from the mean; tables give the area (probability) beyond any z value. Uses: (1) establishing normal (reference) ranges for clinical and laboratory values — usually mean ± 2 SD (covering 95% of healthy population; hence 5% of healthy persons fall outside the 'normal' range); (2) growth charts and anthropometry (Z-scores of weight-for-age, height-for-age, weight-for-height; < −2 SD defines underweight/stunting/wasting, < −3 SD severe); (3) basis of sampling distributions — means of large samples are normally distributed (central limit theorem) — standard error, confidence intervals (mean ± 1.96 SE for 95% CI); (4) basis of parametric tests of significance (z-test, t-test, ANOVA, regression) which assume normality; (5) quality control charts in laboratories (Levey–Jennings); (6) determining probabilities of observations. Departures from normality: skewed distributions (positive — income, hospital stay, triglycerides; negative), kurtosis (leptokurtic — peaked, platykurtic — flat), bimodal; tested by histograms, Q-Q plots, Shapiro–Wilk test; managed by log transformation or non-parametric tests. Other distributions: binomial (discrete — two outcomes), Poisson (rare events), t-distribution (small samples), chi-square distribution.",
[["Characteristics",["Bell-shaped, symmetrical","Mean = median = mode","Asymptotic tails","Total area = 1","Defined by mean and SD"]],
["Area under the curve",[["Range","% observations"],["μ ± 1σ","68.27%"],["μ ± 1.96σ","95%"],["μ ± 2σ","95.45%"],["μ ± 2.58σ","99%"],["μ ± 3σ","99.73%"]]],
["Uses",["Reference ranges (mean ± 2 SD)","Growth chart Z-scores","Confidence intervals","Parametric tests","Quality control"]]],
{c:"The normal distribution, defined by mean and SD with its 68–95–99.7 rule, underpins reference ranges, Z-scores, confidence intervals and parametric statistical tests in medicine."});

Z("cm3",2,"cm",["Sampling is the process of selecting a representative part (sample) of a population to draw conclusions about the whole; methods are probability sampling (simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster, multistage) and non-probability sampling (convenience, purposive, quota, snowball).","Instead of tasting the whole pot, you taste a well-stirred spoonful — random sampling 'stirs the pot' so the spoon represents the whole.",["Simple random sampling: every unit has equal chance — lottery, random number tables, computer; needs sampling frame.","Systematic: every kth unit (k = N/n) after random start; easy; risk with periodicity.","Stratified random: divide population into homogeneous strata (age, sex, rural-urban) then random sample from each (proportionate/disproportionate) — more precise.","Cluster: random selection of groups (villages, schools) and study all units within — e.g., 30-cluster immunisation coverage survey (WHO EPI 30 × 7); multistage (district → block → village → household); multiphase; non-probability — convenience, purposive, quota, snowball (hidden populations)."],"30 × 7 cluster sampling = WHO immunisation coverage survey."],
"Population (universe) is the entire group of individuals or items about which information is required; a sample is a part of the population selected for study; sampling is the process of selecting the sample; sampling frame is the list of all units in the population; sampling unit is the element selected. Advantages of sampling over census: less time, lower cost, fewer personnel, greater accuracy (better trained staff, less fatigue), feasible when population is large or testing is destructive. Requirements of a good sample: representative of the population, adequate size (sample size depends on prevalence, desired precision/allowable error, confidence level, power, variability, design effect), selected by an unbiased method. Sampling errors: (1) sampling error (random error due to chance — reduced by increasing sample size; measured by standard error); (2) non-sampling errors (bias — faulty sampling frame, non-response, measurement errors, observer bias). Methods of sampling: (A) probability (random) sampling — every unit has a known, non-zero chance of selection; allows estimation of sampling error and generalisation: (1) simple random sampling — each unit has an equal chance; lottery method, table of random numbers, computer-generated random numbers; requires complete sampling frame; suitable for small homogeneous populations; (2) systematic random sampling — selecting every kth unit from a list (sampling interval k = N/n) after a random start between 1 and k; simple, spreads sample evenly; danger if the list has periodicity coinciding with k; widely used in household surveys (every 5th house); (3) stratified random sampling — population divided into homogeneous subgroups (strata) based on a characteristic (age, sex, socioeconomic status, rural/urban) and a simple random sample drawn from each stratum, proportionately or disproportionately; gives more representative samples and greater precision for heterogeneous populations; allows comparisons between strata; (4) cluster sampling — the sampling unit is a group (cluster) of elements (village, ward, school, household) rather than individuals; clusters are randomly selected and all (or a sample of) units within selected clusters are studied; useful when sampling frame of individuals is unavailable or population is widely dispersed; cheaper and faster but less precise (design effect ~2); WHO EPI 30-cluster survey for immunisation coverage — 30 clusters selected with probability proportional to size, 7 children (12–23 months) studied per cluster (210 children) to estimate coverage within ±10%; also used in NFHS, nutrition surveys, rapid assessments; (5) multistage sampling — sampling done in stages using different units at each stage (state → district → block → village → household → individual); used in large national surveys (NFHS, NSSO); (6) multiphase sampling — part of the information collected from the whole sample and further information from a subsample (e.g., TB survey — all screened by symptoms, subsample X-ray, further subsample sputum); (7) probability proportional to size; (8) lot quality assurance sampling (LQAS). (B) non-probability sampling — units selected subjectively; cannot estimate sampling error; risk of bias; used in exploratory/qualitative research: (1) convenience (accidental) sampling — readily available subjects (patients attending OPD); (2) purposive (judgemental) sampling — selected by the investigator's judgement as typical or informative; (3) quota sampling — non-random selection to fill quotas in categories (like stratified but non-random); (4) snowball sampling — existing participants recruit further participants (hidden populations — sex workers, drug users); (5) consecutive sampling. Sample size calculation for prevalence: n = Z²pq/L² (Z = 1.96 for 95% confidence, p = prevalence, q = 1 − p, L = allowable error), or 4pq/L².",
[["Probability sampling methods",[["Method","Procedure","Use/advantage"],["Simple random","Equal chance; random numbers/lottery","Small homogeneous populations"],["Systematic","Every kth unit after random start","Easy; household surveys"],["Stratified","Random sample from each stratum","Heterogeneous populations; precision"],["Cluster","Random clusters, all units studied","No frame; dispersed population; 30×7 EPI survey"],["Multistage","Sampling in successive stages","Large national surveys (NFHS)"],["Multiphase","Subsamples for further information","TB surveys"]]],
["Non-probability sampling",[["Method","Description"],["Convenience","Readily available subjects"],["Purposive","Investigator's judgement"],["Quota","Fill category quotas non-randomly"],["Snowball","Participants recruit others (hidden groups)"]]],
["Sample size (prevalence)",["n = Z²pq / L²","Z = 1.96 (95% confidence)","p = expected prevalence, q = 1 − p","L = allowable error","Multiply by design effect for cluster sampling"]]],
{c:"Probability sampling methods — simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage — produce representative samples with measurable error, whereas non-probability methods are quicker but prone to bias; adequate sample size ensures precision."});

Z("cm3",3,"cm",["Tests of significance determine whether an observed difference or association is likely due to chance; the t-test compares means (small samples, quantitative data) and the chi-square test compares proportions/frequencies (qualitative data).","A test of significance is a referee who decides whether the difference between two teams' scores is real or just luck.",["Steps: state null hypothesis (H0 — no difference) and alternative; choose significance level (α = 0.05); calculate test statistic; compare with table value (df); find p-value; accept/reject H0.","Student's t-test (W.S. Gosset): small samples (< 30), quantitative data, normal distribution; unpaired (independent groups) — t = difference of means / SE of difference, df = n1 + n2 − 2; paired (before–after in same subjects) — df = n − 1.","Chi-square test (Karl Pearson): qualitative data, frequencies in contingency tables; χ² = Σ(O − E)²/E; df = (r − 1)(c − 1); expected frequency in each cell ≥ 5 (else Yates' correction/Fisher's exact test).","Others: z-test (large samples, means/proportions), ANOVA (> 2 means), correlation and regression, non-parametric — Mann–Whitney, Wilcoxon, Kruskal–Wallis."],"Means → t-test (z if large); proportions → chi-square."],
"Tests of significance are statistical procedures to decide whether an observed difference between samples (or between sample and population) is real or could have occurred by chance (sampling variation). Basic concepts: null hypothesis (H0) — there is no real difference (difference due to chance); alternative hypothesis (H1) — a real difference exists; level of significance (α) — probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis (type I error), conventionally 5% (0.05) or 1%; p-value — probability of obtaining the observed (or more extreme) result if H0 is true; p < 0.05 → reject H0 (statistically significant); degrees of freedom; one-tailed vs two-tailed tests; confidence intervals provide magnitude and precision. Steps in testing: (1) state the null and alternative hypotheses; (2) choose the appropriate test according to type of data (quantitative/qualitative), sample size, number of groups, paired or independent, distribution; (3) set the level of significance; (4) calculate the test statistic; (5) determine degrees of freedom and compare with critical (table) value or obtain p-value; (6) draw conclusion — reject or fail to reject H0; (7) interpret clinically (statistical vs clinical significance). Choice of tests: parametric tests (assume normal distribution, quantitative data) — z-test (standard error of difference between means or proportions; large samples ≥ 30), Student's t-test (small samples), ANOVA (F-test; comparing means of three or more groups), Pearson correlation, linear regression; non-parametric tests (distribution-free; ordinal data or non-normal) — chi-square test, Mann–Whitney U test (alternative to unpaired t), Wilcoxon signed-rank test (paired t), Kruskal–Wallis test (ANOVA), Spearman rank correlation, Fisher's exact test, McNemar test (paired proportions). Student's t-test (W.S. Gosset, pen-name 'Student', 1908): applied to compare means when sample size is small (< 30) and population SD unknown, with quantitative data approximately normally distributed and similar variances; (a) unpaired (independent) t-test — compares means of two independent groups (e.g., Hb in boys vs girls; drug vs placebo groups): t = (x̄1 − x̄2)/SE(x̄1 − x̄2), where SE = pooled SD × √(1/n1 + 1/n2); degrees of freedom = n1 + n2 − 2; (b) paired t-test — compares means of paired observations on the same individuals before and after intervention (e.g., BP before and after a drug) or matched pairs: t = mean difference (d̄)/(SD of differences/√n); df = n − 1; (c) one-sample t-test — sample mean vs known population mean; calculated t compared with table t at given df and significance level; if calculated t > table t, the difference is significant. Chi-square test (χ², Karl Pearson, 1900): non-parametric test applied to qualitative (categorical) data expressed as frequencies or proportions in contingency tables; uses — (1) test of association between two attributes (e.g., smoking and lung cancer; vaccination and attack), (2) test of proportions (comparing proportions in two or more groups), (3) goodness of fit (observed vs expected distribution); calculation — construct contingency table of observed frequencies (O); calculate expected frequencies (E) for each cell = (row total × column total)/grand total under H0; χ² = Σ[(O − E)²/E]; degrees of freedom = (rows − 1)(columns − 1) (for 2×2 table df = 1; critical value at 5% = 3.84); shortcut for 2×2: χ² = N(ad − bc)²/[(a+b)(c+d)(a+c)(b+d)]; conditions — random samples, independent observations, data as frequencies (not percentages), total sample reasonably large (> 40), expected frequency in each cell ≥ 5 (if < 5, apply Yates' continuity correction or Fisher's exact test); limitations — tells association, not strength or causation. Other tests: z-test for proportions (large samples), ANOVA with post-hoc tests, correlation coefficient (r, −1 to +1), regression, survival analysis (log-rank test).",
[["Choice of test",[["Data","Two groups","Paired","> 2 groups"],["Quantitative, normal","Unpaired t-test (z if large)","Paired t-test","ANOVA"],["Quantitative, non-normal/ordinal","Mann–Whitney U","Wilcoxon signed-rank","Kruskal–Wallis"],["Qualitative (frequencies)","Chi-square / Fisher's exact","McNemar","Chi-square"]]],
["t-test vs chi-square",[["Feature","t-test","Chi-square"],["Data","Quantitative (means)","Qualitative (frequencies)"],["Type","Parametric","Non-parametric"],["Sample","Small (< 30)","Any (expected ≥ 5 per cell)"],["Formula","Difference of means / SE","Σ(O − E)²/E"],["df","n1 + n2 − 2 (unpaired); n − 1 (paired)","(r − 1)(c − 1)"],["Example","Mean Hb in two groups","Smoking vs lung cancer"]]],
["Steps of testing",["State H0 and H1","Choose test and α (0.05)","Calculate statistic","Find df, compare with table / p-value","Reject or retain H0","Interpret clinically"]]],
{c:"Tests of significance assess whether differences arise by chance; Student's t-test compares means of small quantitative samples (paired or unpaired), while the chi-square test examines associations between categorical variables using observed and expected frequencies."});

Z("cm3",4,"cm",["A type I error (α) is rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive — concluding a difference exists when it does not); a type II error (β) is failing to reject a false null hypothesis (false negative — missing a real difference). The p-value is the probability of obtaining the observed result if the null hypothesis were true; power = 1 − β.","Type I is a false alarm (convicting an innocent person); type II is a missed detection (letting a guilty person go free).",["Type I error (α): significance level, usually 0.05; reduced by lowering α (0.01) — but increases β.","Type II error (β): usually 0.1–0.2; power (1 − β) = probability of detecting a true difference (80–90%); increase power by larger sample size, larger effect size, lower variability, higher α.","p-value: p < 0.05 → statistically significant (reject H0); p is not the probability that H0 is true; does not indicate size or clinical importance — report confidence intervals.","Confidence interval (95%): range likely to contain the true value; if 95% CI of difference excludes 0 (or of RR/OR excludes 1) → significant."],"Type I = α = false positive; Type II = β = false negative; Power = 1 − β."],
"In hypothesis testing, decisions are made on sample data and can be wrong. Null hypothesis (H0): there is no difference/association; alternative hypothesis (H1): there is a difference. Four possible outcomes: (1) H0 true and accepted — correct decision (probability 1 − α, confidence level); (2) H0 true but rejected — type I error (α error, false positive) — concluding that a difference or effect exists when in fact it does not (e.g., declaring an ineffective drug effective); the probability of type I error is the level of significance (α), fixed by the investigator before the study, conventionally 0.05 (5%) or 0.01; (3) H0 false but accepted (not rejected) — type II error (β error, false negative) — concluding no difference when a real difference exists (e.g., failing to detect that an effective drug works); conventionally β is set at 0.10–0.20; (4) H0 false and rejected — correct decision; probability = power of the test (1 − β), commonly 80% or 90%. Relationship: for a given sample size, decreasing α increases β and vice versa; both can be reduced only by increasing the sample size. Factors increasing power (reducing type II error): larger sample size, larger true effect size (difference to be detected), smaller variability (SD), higher α (less stringent), one-tailed test, paired design, better measurement precision. Sample size calculation requires specifying α, power (1 − β), expected effect size and variability. Analogies: courtroom (type I — convicting an innocent; type II — acquitting the guilty), screening (type I — false positive; type II — false negative). P-value: the probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true; small p-value (< α, usually 0.05) indicates that the observed result is unlikely under H0 — H0 is rejected and the result is called statistically significant; p > 0.05 — not significant (insufficient evidence to reject H0; does not prove H0 true — 'absence of evidence is not evidence of absence', may reflect inadequate power); common misinterpretations — p is not the probability that H0 is true, nor the probability that the result is due to chance alone, nor a measure of effect size or clinical importance; very large samples may produce statistically significant but clinically trivial differences; multiple comparisons increase the chance of type I error (Bonferroni correction). Confidence intervals: a range of values within which the true population value is likely to lie with a specified probability (95% CI = estimate ± 1.96 SE); provide information on both magnitude and precision of an estimate; a 95% CI for a difference that excludes zero (or for a ratio — RR, OR — that excludes 1) corresponds to p < 0.05; narrower CI with larger samples. Statistical significance should always be interpreted alongside clinical significance (effect size, NNT), study design, biases and confounding.",
[["Decision table",[["Decision","H0 true","H0 false"],["Reject H0","Type I error (α) — false positive","Correct (power = 1 − β)"],["Accept H0","Correct (1 − α)","Type II error (β) — false negative"]]],
["Comparison",[["Feature","Type I error","Type II error"],["Also called","α error, false positive","β error, false negative"],["Meaning","Difference found when none exists","Real difference missed"],["Usual value","0.05","0.1–0.2"],["Reduced by","Lower α","Larger sample, larger effect"],["Related to","Significance level","Power (1 − β)"]]],
["Interpreting p-value",["p < 0.05: statistically significant (reject H0)","p ≥ 0.05: not significant (cannot reject H0)","Not probability that H0 is true","Does not indicate effect size — report 95% CI","Clinical significance assessed separately"]]],
{c:"Type I and type II errors are the false-positive and false-negative risks of hypothesis testing, controlled by α and power; the p-value indicates compatibility with the null hypothesis, and confidence intervals add magnitude and precision for clinically meaningful interpretation."});

Z("cm3",5,"cm",["Statistical data are presented in tables and graphs/diagrams: histograms and frequency polygons for continuous quantitative data, bar charts and pie charts for qualitative/discrete data, line diagrams for trends over time, and scatter diagrams for correlation.","Choosing a chart is like choosing a container — pour continuous data into histograms, categories into bars or pies, and time trends into lines.",["Tabulation: simple and complex (frequency distribution); title, number, headings, units, footnotes, source.","Histogram: continuous quantitative data (age, Hb); adjacent rectangles (no gaps), area ∝ frequency; frequency polygon joins midpoints; ogive (cumulative frequency — median/percentiles).","Bar diagram: qualitative/discrete data; bars separated by gaps; simple, multiple (compound), component (subdivided/proportional); pie chart: proportions of a whole (sector angle = % × 3.6°).","Line diagram: trend over time (IMR over years); scatter diagram: relationship between two quantitative variables (correlation); others — pictogram, map/spot map (geographical distribution), box-and-whisker plot, stem-and-leaf."],"Histogram = continuous (no gaps); bar chart = categories (gaps)."],
"Presentation of data makes large masses of data understandable, highlights important features, facilitates comparison and further analysis. Methods: (A) tabulation — the most common method; tables should be numbered, have a clear concise title (what, where, when), headings of columns and rows with units, data arranged logically (alphabetically, chronologically, by size), totals, footnotes and source; types — simple table (one variable — frequency distribution) and complex table (two or more variables — cross-tabulation); frequency distribution table — data grouped into class intervals (usually 5–15 classes, equal width, mutually exclusive) with frequencies. (B) charts and diagrams — visual impression, easy comprehension, but less detail: for quantitative continuous data — (1) histogram: pictorial diagram of frequency distribution of continuous data; class intervals on X-axis and frequency on Y-axis; adjacent rectangles with no gaps; area of each rectangle proportional to frequency (if class intervals unequal, height adjusted); shows shape (normal, skewed); (2) frequency polygon: obtained by joining midpoints of the tops of histogram rectangles with straight lines; useful to compare two or more distributions on same graph; (3) frequency curve (smoothed polygon with large data); (4) cumulative frequency diagram (ogive): cumulative frequencies plotted against upper class limits — used to find median, quartiles, percentiles (growth charts); (5) box-and-whisker plot (median, quartiles, range, outliers); (6) stem-and-leaf plot; for qualitative (categorical) or discrete data — (7) bar diagram (bar chart): bars of equal width with gaps between them, length proportional to frequency; types — simple bar (one variable — e.g., causes of death), multiple/compound bar (comparison of two or more variables side by side — e.g., IMR in states by year), component (subdivided) bar (bar divided into components — e.g., male/female), proportional (100%) component bar; horizontal or vertical; (8) pie chart (sector diagram): circle divided into sectors whose areas (angles) are proportional to frequencies — angle = (frequency/total) × 360° (or % × 3.6°); shows proportional distribution of parts of a whole (e.g., causes of maternal deaths); limited to few categories; (9) pictogram: pictures/symbols to represent frequency — popular for lay audiences; for time trends — (10) line diagram (line graph): shows trends of events over time (e.g., trend of IMR, incidence of malaria over years); time on X-axis, frequency/rate on Y-axis; several lines can be compared; semi-logarithmic scale for rates of change; for relationship — (11) scatter diagram (dot diagram/correlation diagram): two quantitative variables plotted as dots to show correlation (positive, negative, none) — e.g., height and weight; regression line; for geographical data — (12) map diagrams (cartograms) and spot maps — show geographical distribution of cases (John Snow's cholera map; outbreak investigation), shaded/choropleth maps; others — epidemic curve (histogram of cases by time of onset), population pyramid (age–sex structure), Gantt charts, forest plots (meta-analysis), survival curves (Kaplan–Meier). General principles: title, scale, labelled axes with units, legends/keys, source, simplicity, avoid distortion (Y-axis starting at zero for bars), choose appropriate type.",
[["Choice of diagram",[["Data type","Diagram"],["Continuous quantitative (frequency distribution)","Histogram, frequency polygon, ogive, box plot"],["Qualitative / discrete","Bar diagram (simple, multiple, component), pie chart, pictogram"],["Trend over time","Line diagram"],["Relationship between two variables","Scatter diagram"],["Geographical distribution","Spot map, choropleth map"],["Outbreak","Epidemic curve"],["Age–sex structure","Population pyramid"]]],
["Histogram vs bar chart",[["Feature","Histogram","Bar chart"],["Data","Continuous quantitative","Qualitative/discrete"],["Gaps between bars","No","Yes"],["Area vs height","Area ∝ frequency","Height ∝ frequency"],["Order on X-axis","Fixed (numerical)","Any"]]]],
{c:"Data are best presented with tables and appropriate diagrams — histograms and polygons for continuous data, bar and pie charts for categories, line graphs for trends, scatter diagrams for correlation and maps for geography."});

Z("cm4",0,"cm",["Tuberculosis, caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis and spread by airborne droplet nuclei, is India's largest infectious disease burden; the National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) aims to end TB by 2025 (ahead of SDG 2030) through early diagnosis by NAAT, daily fixed-dose treatment, patient support (Nikshay Poshan Yojana) and prevention.","TB control is 'Detect – Treat – Prevent – Build': find every case, treat everyone to cure, protect contacts, and build systems and nutrition support.",["Epidemiology: India ~28 lakh cases/year (27% of global), incidence ~195/lakh (2022, ↓), mortality ~23/lakh; source — sputum-positive pulmonary case; one untreated case infects 10–15/year; risk factors — HIV, malnutrition, diabetes, smoking, alcohol, overcrowding, silicosis.","Prevention: BCG at birth, TB preventive therapy (6H or 3HP) for contacts < 5 years, all household contacts, PLHIV; airborne infection control, nutrition, case finding (active case finding in vulnerable groups).","NTEP (renamed 2020 from RNTCP, 1997; NTP 1962): National Strategic Plan 2017–25 (Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build); diagnosis — CBNAAT/Truenat upfront with rifampicin resistance; universal DST; treatment — 2HRZE/4HRE daily FDC; MDR — BPaLM/shorter oral regimens; Nikshay portal, Nikshay Poshan Yojana (₹500/month → ₹1000 from 2024), private sector engagement (PPSA), Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan (Ni-kshay Mitra), TB-free panchayats.","Indicators: presumptive TB examination rate, notification rate, treatment success rate (> 90%), case definitions — microbiologically confirmed/clinically diagnosed; outcomes — cured, treatment completed, failed, lost to follow-up, died."],"NTEP pillars: 'Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build' (NSP 2017–25)."],
"Tuberculosis is a chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex (M. tuberculosis, M. bovis), primarily affecting the lungs. Global and Indian burden: WHO Global TB Report 2023 — 10.6 million cases and 1.3 million deaths globally; India accounts for ~27% of global cases (~28 lakh incident cases; incidence 199/lakh in 2022, a 16% decline from 2015), highest MDR-TB burden (~1.1 lakh); TB-HIV co-infection ~2–3%. Epidemiological determinants: agent — acid-fast bacilli, aerobic, slow-growing; survive in dark, moist places for months; killed by sunlight, heat (pasteurisation); source of infection — human cases with open (sputum smear/NAAT positive) pulmonary TB (most infectious), bovine source (unpasteurised milk — M. bovis); infectivity — one untreated sputum-positive case infects 10–15 persons per year; communicability until treatment (rapidly reduced within 2 weeks of effective therapy); host factors — age (all ages; peak in young adults and elderly), sex (males > females), heredity, nutrition (malnutrition increases risk — population attributable fraction ~35–40% in India), immunosuppression (HIV — 20-fold risk; diabetes 3-fold; steroids; cancer; silicosis; alcohol; smoking; chronic kidney disease), social factors — poverty, overcrowding, poor ventilation, illiteracy, migration, urbanisation, lack of awareness, stigma; mode of transmission — airborne droplet nuclei (1–5 µm) generated by coughing, sneezing, talking; inhalation; ingestion (bovine TB); rarely inoculation; incubation — 3–6 weeks for primary infection (tuberculin conversion); lifetime risk of disease among infected ~5–10% (higher in first 2 years; HIV ~10% per year); latent TB infection in about one-quarter of the world's population (~35–40% in India). Control strategies: (1) case finding — passive (symptomatic attending facilities — presumptive TB: cough ≥ 2 weeks, fever, weight loss, night sweats, haemoptysis, any abnormality on chest X-ray) and active case finding in vulnerable groups (slums, tribal, prisons, contacts, PLHIV, diabetics, malnourished); diagnosis — upfront molecular testing (CBNAAT/GeneXpert, Truenat — detects MTB and rifampicin resistance), sputum smear microscopy (Ziehl–Neelsen/fluorescence LED), chest X-ray, culture and drug susceptibility testing (liquid culture, LPA), universal DST; extrapulmonary — appropriate specimens; (2) treatment — free drugs; daily regimen with weight-band fixed-dose combinations; drug-sensitive TB: 2HRZE + 4HRE (6 months), extended for CNS and osteoarticular TB; drug-resistant TB: H-mono/poly-resistant regimen, shorter oral bedaquiline-containing MDR/RR-TB regimen, BPaLM (6 months) since 2024; treatment adherence — community DOT, 99DOTS, MERM boxes, family DOT; (3) prevention — BCG vaccination at birth (protects infants against severe forms), TB preventive treatment (TPT) — 6H (isoniazid 6 months) or 3HP (rifapentine + INH weekly × 12) or 4R for household contacts of all ages (after ruling out active TB), PLHIV, and other risk groups; airborne infection control in health facilities (triage, cough etiquette, ventilation, N95 masks); addressing social determinants (nutrition, housing); (4) nutritional support — Nikshay Poshan Yojana (direct benefit transfer of ₹500 per month during treatment, increased to ₹1000 in 2024), food baskets via Ni-kshay Mitras under Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan (2022). History of programme: National TB Programme (1962; district TB centres, BCG), Revised National TB Control Programme (1997; DOTS strategy — political commitment, sputum microscopy, standardised short-course chemotherapy under direct observation, uninterrupted drug supply, recording and reporting), daily regimen (2017), renamed National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP) in January 2020. National Strategic Plan for TB Elimination 2017–2025: goal — End TB by 2025 (five years ahead of SDG target 2030): reduce incidence by 80% and mortality by 90% compared with 2015, zero catastrophic cost; strategic pillars 'Detect – Treat – Prevent – Build (DTPB)': Detect — find all TB patients with emphasis on reaching TB patients seeking care from private providers and undiagnosed TB in high-risk populations (private sector engagement through Patient Provider Support Agencies, mandatory notification since 2012, active case finding); Treat — initiate and sustain all patients on appropriate anti-TB treatment wrapped around with patient-friendly systems and social support (Nikshay Poshan, treatment supporters, incentives to private providers, DBT); Prevent — prevent emergence of TB in susceptible populations (contact tracing, TPT, airborne infection control, BCG, addressing social determinants); Build — build and strengthen enabling policies, empowered institutions, additional human resources, multisectoral response (Nikshay case-based web portal, TB Harega Desh Jeetega campaign, TB Mukt Panchayat initiative, research — ICMR, indigenous diagnostics). Monitoring indicators: presumptive TB examination rate, notification rate (public and private), proportion tested for rifampicin resistance, treatment success rate (target > 90%), TPT coverage, Nikshay Poshan beneficiaries, case fatality. Definitions: presumptive TB, microbiologically confirmed TB, clinically diagnosed TB, new and previously treated cases; treatment outcomes — cured (bacteriologically confirmed, negative at end), treatment completed, treatment failed, died, lost to follow-up (interruption ≥ 1 month), not evaluated, treatment regimen changed; treatment success = cured + completed.",
[["Epidemiology",[["Factor","Detail"],["Agent","M. tuberculosis (AFB), M. bovis"],["Source","Sputum-positive pulmonary case"],["Transmission","Airborne droplet nuclei"],["Infectivity","10–15 persons/year per untreated case"],["Risk factors","HIV, malnutrition, diabetes, smoking, alcohol, overcrowding, silicosis"],["Lifetime risk (infected)","5–10%"],["India burden","~27% of global cases; highest MDR burden"]]],
["NTEP components",[["Component","Activities"],["Diagnosis","NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat) upfront, universal DST, CXR, ACF"],["Treatment","2HRZE/4HRE daily FDC; BPaLM/shorter oral MDR regimens"],["Adherence","Community DOT, 99DOTS, Nikshay"],["Prevention","BCG, TPT (6H/3HP), infection control"],["Support","Nikshay Poshan Yojana, Ni-kshay Mitra, DBT"],["Private sector","Mandatory notification, PPSA"],["Monitoring","Nikshay portal; treatment success > 90%"]]],
["Treatment outcomes",["Cured","Treatment completed","Treatment failed","Died","Lost to follow-up","Not evaluated","Regimen changed"]]],
{i:"India aims to eliminate TB by 2025, five years ahead of the global SDG target, through the NSP 2017–25.",x:[["History",["NTP 1962","RNTCP 1997 (DOTS)","Daily regimen 2017","NTEP 2020","PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan 2022"]]],f:"Source (sputum + case) → droplet nuclei → infection (latent; ~35% Indians) → disease (5–10%; ↑ HIV, malnutrition, diabetes)\n NTEP: Detect (NAAT) → Treat (FDC, DOT, Nikshay Poshan) → Prevent (BCG, TPT, IC) → Build (Nikshay, private sector, panchayats)",c:"Tuberculosis remains India's leading infectious killer; NTEP's Detect–Treat–Prevent–Build strategy, with molecular diagnosis, daily FDC regimens, TPT, nutritional support and private sector engagement, drives the goal of ending TB."});

Z("cm4",1,"cm",["Malaria is a vector-borne disease caused by Plasmodium species and transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes; India's National Centre for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC) aims for malaria elimination by 2030 through early diagnosis and complete treatment, vector control (IRS, LLINs), surveillance and community participation.","Malaria control attacks three links: the parasite in humans (test and treat), the mosquito (spraying, nets, larval control) and the human–mosquito contact (personal protection).",["Epidemiology: P. vivax and P. falciparum (~ equal; Pf more in Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, NE); vectors — An. culicifacies (rural), An. stephensi (urban), An. fluviatilis (hills), An. minimus, An. dirus (NE), An. sundaicus (coastal); cases fell from ~20 lakh (2000s) to ~2 lakh (2023).","Indicators: API (annual parasite incidence = positives/population × 1000), ABER (annual blood examination rate ≥ 10%), SPR (slide positivity rate), SFR, Pf%; stratification for elimination — category 0–3 (API).","NFME 2016–2030: phases — eliminate in category 1 states by 2022, all by 2027, prevent re-establishment by 2030; strategies — diagnosis by microscopy/bivalent RDT, treatment (Pv: CQ + PQ 14 days; Pf: ACT AS+SP / AL in NE + PQ single), IRS (DDT, synthetic pyrethroids), LLINs, larval source management, Gambusia/guppy fish, biolarvicides, IEC, ASHA involvement.","Surveillance: active (fortnightly by MPW/ASHA), passive; 1-3-7 strategy in elimination (notify in 1 day, investigate in 3, respond in 7)."],"Malaria indicators: API, ABER (≥ 10%), SPR, SFR."],
"Malaria is a protozoal disease caused by Plasmodium vivax, P. falciparum, P. malariae, P. ovale (and zoonotic P. knowlesi), transmitted by the bite of infected female Anopheles mosquitoes. Burden: global ~249 million cases and 6 lakh deaths (2022, mostly Africa); India accounts for most of the South-East Asia region burden but has achieved a large decline (~85% reduction since 2015; ~2 lakh confirmed cases and < 100 deaths reported in 2023), with focal high transmission in tribal, forested and border areas (Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Tripura, MP); P. falciparum ~ 50% of cases; India exited WHO's High Burden to High Impact group in 2024. Epidemiological determinants: agent — Plasmodium life cycle with asexual (schizogony) phase in man and sexual (sporogony) phase in mosquito; extrinsic incubation period 10–14 days (temperature-dependent); intrinsic incubation period — Pv 14 days, Pf 12 days, Pm 28 days; hypnozoites (Pv, Po) cause relapses; drug resistance — chloroquine-resistant Pf widespread, emerging artemisinin partial resistance in NE; host — all ages and both sexes susceptible; high risk — children, pregnant women (severe malaria, low birth weight), non-immune migrants, tribal populations, forest workers; innate resistance — sickle cell trait, thalassaemia, G6PD deficiency, Duffy-negative (Pv); environment — temperature 20–30 °C, relative humidity > 60%, rainfall (monsoon), altitude (< 2000 m), water collections (breeding), deforestation, irrigation projects, urbanisation (construction sites, overhead tanks — An. stephensi), population movement; vectors — main Indian vectors: An. culicifacies (rural plains, ~65% cases, resistant to DDT/malathion), An. stephensi (urban malaria; breeds in wells, overhead tanks, construction sites), An. fluviatilis (foothills, streams), An. minimus and An. baimaii/dirus (NE forests — highly efficient), An. sundaicus (coastal brackish water — Andaman), An. philippinensis; mode of transmission — vector-borne (inoculation of sporozoites), direct (blood transfusion, sharing needles, congenital). Malariometric measures: (a) parasitological indices — annual parasite incidence (API = confirmed cases during one year/population under surveillance × 1000), annual blood examination rate (ABER = number of blood smears examined in a year/population × 100; ≥ 10% recommended for adequate surveillance), slide positivity rate (SPR — percentage of slides examined found positive), slide falciparum rate (SFR), Pf percentage, infant parasite rate (most sensitive index of recent transmission), parasite density index; (b) clinical — spleen rate, average enlarged spleen (in children 2–9 years); (c) entomological — man-hour density, human blood index, sporozoite rate, entomological inoculation rate; (d) immunological. Programme history: National Malaria Control Programme (1953) → National Malaria Eradication Programme (1958; near-eradication with ~1 lakh cases by 1965 then resurgence in 1970s due to insecticide resistance, administrative and financial problems) → Modified Plan of Operation (1977; API-based stratification, drug distribution centres, fever treatment depots) → Urban Malaria Scheme (1971) → Malaria Action Programme (1995) → Enhanced Malaria Control Project (1997) → National Anti-Malaria Programme (1999) → National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (2003; merged malaria, filaria, kala-azar, JE, dengue, chikungunya) → renamed National Centre for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC). National Framework for Malaria Elimination (NFME) 2016–2030 and National Strategic Plan 2023–27: vision — malaria-free India by 2030; objectives — eliminate malaria (zero indigenous cases) throughout the country by 2030 and maintain malaria-free status in areas where transmission has been interrupted; states/districts stratified by API into category 0 (prevention of re-establishment), category 1 (elimination phase — API < 1 in all districts), category 2 (pre-elimination — API < 1 overall but some districts ≥ 1), category 3 (intensified control — API ≥ 1); targets — category 1 elimination by 2022, all states API < 1 by 2024, interruption of indigenous transmission nationally by 2027, prevention of re-establishment by 2030. Strategies: (1) diagnosis and case management — early diagnosis by microscopy or bivalent rapid diagnostic tests (Pf/Pv) at all levels including ASHAs in the community, complete radical treatment within 24 h (vivax: chloroquine 3 days + primaquine 14 days; falciparum: ACT — artesunate + sulfadoxine–pyrimethamine for 3 days (artemether–lumefantrine in NE states) + single-dose primaquine; severe malaria — parenteral artesunate, referral); (2) surveillance — active (fortnightly house-to-house fever surveillance by MPW/ASHA), passive (health facilities), sentinel and mass surveys; case-based surveillance with case investigation, classification (indigenous, imported, relapse, introduced, induced) and foci investigation in elimination areas — '1-3-7' approach (case notification within 1 day, investigation within 3 days, focus response within 7 days); (3) integrated vector management — indoor residual spraying (IRS) with DDT, malathion or synthetic pyrethroids (alpha-cypermethrin, deltamethrin, lambda-cyhalothrin) in two rounds in high-risk areas, long-lasting insecticidal nets (LLINs) distributed free in high-endemic areas, larval source management (anti-larval measures — temephos, biolarvicides — Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis, larvivorous fish Gambusia and guppy, source reduction — filling, draining, removal of water collections), environmental management, urban by-laws for construction sites; insecticide resistance monitoring; (4) personal protection — repellents, mosquito nets, screening, clothing; (5) behaviour change communication — IEC, community mobilisation, World Malaria Day (25 April), anti-malaria month (June); (6) intersectoral collaboration, cross-border initiatives, private sector engagement; (7) epidemic preparedness and rapid response; (8) monitoring and evaluation — IHIP/MIS, drug and insecticide resistance monitoring; (9) research — vaccines (RTS,S/AS01 and R21/Matrix-M approved by WHO for children in Africa), new drugs (tafenoquine).",
[["Indian vectors",[["Vector","Habitat"],["An. culicifacies","Rural plains (main vector)"],["An. stephensi","Urban (wells, tanks, construction)"],["An. fluviatilis","Foothills, streams"],["An. minimus, An. baimaii (dirus)","North-East forests"],["An. sundaicus","Coastal brackish water"]]],
["Malariometric indices",[["Index","Formula / meaning"],["API","Confirmed cases/year ÷ population × 1000"],["ABER","Slides examined/year ÷ population × 100 (≥ 10%)"],["SPR","Positive slides ÷ slides examined × 100"],["SFR","Pf-positive slides ÷ slides examined × 100"],["Infant parasite rate","Most sensitive index of recent transmission"],["Spleen rate","% children 2–9 yr with enlarged spleen"]]],
["NFME strategies",[["Strategy","Interventions"],["Case management","Microscopy/RDT; ACT for Pf, CQ + PQ for Pv; parenteral artesunate for severe"],["Surveillance","Active (fortnightly), passive; case-based; 1-3-7"],["Vector control","IRS, LLINs, larval source management, Gambusia/guppy, Bti"],["Personal protection","Nets, repellents, screening"],["IEC/BCC","Community mobilisation, ASHA"],["Elimination targets","Zero indigenous cases by 2027; malaria-free by 2030"]]]],
{i:"India's malaria burden has declined sharply, and the programme has shifted from control to elimination with focus on tribal and forested hotspots.",x:[["Programme history",["NMCP 1953 → NMEP 1958 → MPO 1977 → NAMP 1999 → NVBDCP 2003 → NCVBDC","NFME 2016–2030; NSP 2023–27"]]],f:"Infected human (gametocytes) → Anopheles bite → sporogony (10–14 days) → bite → sporozoites → liver → blood schizogony (fever)\n Control: test & treat (ACT/CQ+PQ) | IRS, LLINs, larval control | personal protection | surveillance (1-3-7)",c:"Malaria control in India rests on early diagnosis and complete treatment, integrated vector management with IRS and LLINs, robust surveillance using API and ABER, and community participation, with the NFME targeting elimination by 2030."});

Z("cm4",2,"cm",["Dengue is a viral (flavivirus, 4 serotypes) disease transmitted by day-biting Aedes aegypti mosquitoes breeding in clean stored water; it ranges from fever to severe dengue with plasma leakage, bleeding and shock; control focuses on source reduction, early case management and community action.","Aedes is a 'domestic' mosquito that breeds in clean water in coolers, tyres and pots and bites in the daytime — so control starts at home: empty, cover, dry.",["Agent: dengue virus DENV 1–4; secondary infection with a different serotype → severe dengue (antibody-dependent enhancement).","Vector: Aedes aegypti (urban, daytime biter, breeds in artificial containers — coolers, tyres, flower pots, overhead tanks), Ae. albopictus (peri-urban); transovarial transmission; EIP 8–10 days; IP 3–14 (4–7) days.","Classification (WHO 2009): dengue without warning signs, with warning signs (abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleed, lethargy, hepatomegaly, rising HCT with falling platelets), severe dengue (plasma leakage → shock, severe bleeding, organ impairment); diagnosis — NS1 antigen (day 1–5), IgM ELISA (after day 5).","Control (NCVBDC): no specific antiviral (vaccine — Dengvaxia/Qdenga not in programme); fluids, paracetamol (avoid NSAIDs/aspirin); vector control — source reduction ('dry day' weekly), larval indices (house index, container index, Breteau index > 50 = high risk), temephos, fogging/space spraying during outbreaks, personal protection; National Dengue Day 16 May."],"Aedes indices: House index, Container index, Breteau index (containers +/100 houses)."],
"Dengue is the most rapidly spreading mosquito-borne viral disease in the world, endemic in most Indian states with large urban outbreaks (post-monsoon, August–November); India reports 1–3 lakh confirmed cases annually (under-reported). Agent: dengue virus, a single-stranded RNA flavivirus with four antigenically distinct serotypes (DENV-1 to DENV-4); infection confers lifelong immunity to the infecting serotype but only short-term cross-protection; secondary infection with a heterologous serotype increases risk of severe dengue via antibody-dependent enhancement. Vector: Aedes aegypti — principal urban vector; small black mosquito with white stripes ('tiger mosquito'); day-biter (peak early morning and late afternoon); anthropophilic, endophilic; breeds in clean, stagnant water in artificial containers (desert coolers, overhead tanks, discarded tyres, flower vases, plant pots, buckets, bottles, coconut shells, construction sites); eggs resist desiccation for months; flight range ~100–400 m; transovarial transmission maintains virus; Aedes albopictus — peri-urban/rural, breeds in natural and artificial containers; extrinsic incubation period 8–10 days; mosquito remains infective for life. Host: all ages; infants and children at higher risk of severe disease in hyperendemic areas; incubation period 3–14 days (usually 4–7); viraemia from 1 day before to 5 days after onset (period of infectivity to mosquitoes). Environment: rainfall, temperature 25–30 °C, humidity, urbanisation, water storage practices, poor solid waste management, population movement. Clinical spectrum (WHO 2009 classification): (1) dengue without warning signs — fever with two of nausea/vomiting, rash, aches and pains (retro-orbital pain, myalgia, arthralgia — 'breakbone fever'), positive tourniquet test, leucopenia; (2) dengue with warning signs — abdominal pain or tenderness, persistent vomiting, clinical fluid accumulation (ascites, pleural effusion), mucosal bleeding, lethargy/restlessness, liver enlargement > 2 cm, increase in haematocrit concurrent with rapid decrease in platelet count — require hospital admission; (3) severe dengue — severe plasma leakage leading to shock (dengue shock syndrome) or fluid accumulation with respiratory distress; severe bleeding; severe organ impairment (liver AST/ALT ≥ 1000, CNS, heart); phases — febrile (2–7 days), critical (defervescence; days 3–7 — plasma leakage), recovery; older classification — dengue fever, dengue haemorrhagic fever grades I–IV, dengue shock syndrome. Diagnosis: NS1 antigen ELISA/RDT (days 1–5), IgM capture ELISA (after day 5), IgG (secondary), RT-PCR (serotyping); platelets and haematocrit monitoring. Case management: no specific antiviral; paracetamol for fever (avoid aspirin, ibuprofen, other NSAIDs and IM injections — bleeding), oral fluids, rest; close monitoring of warning signs; IV crystalloids titrated by haematocrit and haemodynamics during critical phase; blood transfusion for significant bleeding; platelet transfusion only when < 10,000 with bleeding (not prophylactically); case fatality < 1% with good care. Prevention and control (National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme / NCVBDC; Mid-term plan for prevention and control of dengue and chikungunya; Long-term action plan): (1) disease surveillance — sentinel surveillance hospitals with laboratory support (ELISA), Apex Referral Laboratories, IDSP/IHIP reporting, outbreak response; (2) case management — guidelines, training, hospital preparedness; (3) integrated vector management — (a) entomological surveillance using Aedes larval indices: house index (HI — percentage of houses infested with larvae/pupae), container index (CI — percentage of water-holding containers positive), Breteau index (BI — number of positive containers per 100 houses inspected; BI > 50 indicates high risk of transmission, < 5 low risk), pupal index; (b) source reduction (most important) — elimination of breeding sites: emptying, cleaning and drying water containers at least once a week ('dry day' — every Sunday), covering water storage tanks, proper disposal of solid waste (tyres, containers), desert cooler maintenance, building by-laws; (c) anti-larval measures — temephos (Abate) 1 ppm in potable water containers, biolarvicides (Bti), larvivorous fish (guppy, Gambusia) in tanks and wells; (d) adult control — space spraying/thermal fogging and ultra-low volume (ULV) spraying with pyrethrum/malathion during outbreaks, focal spraying around cases (within 50–100 m); (4) personal protection — full-sleeved clothing, mosquito repellents (DEET), insecticide-treated curtains and nets (also for daytime sleeping of infants and patients — prevent mosquitoes biting cases), window screens; (5) behaviour change communication — community participation (COMBI — Communication for Behavioural Impact), school programmes, National Dengue Day (16 May), ASHA involvement; (6) intersectoral coordination — municipal corporations, urban development, education, railways; (7) vaccines — CYD-TDV (Dengvaxia — only for seropositive individuals), TAK-003 (Qdenga), Butantan-DV; Indian vaccines (DengiAll) under trial; Wolbachia-infected Aedes release strategies in some countries.",
[["Epidemiology",[["Factor","Detail"],["Agent","Dengue virus (flavivirus), DENV 1–4"],["Vector","Aedes aegypti (urban), Ae. albopictus"],["Biting","Daytime"],["Breeding","Clean water in artificial containers"],["EIP / IP","8–10 days / 3–14 days (4–7)"],["Severe disease","Secondary heterologous infection (ADE)"]]],
["WHO 2009 classification",[["Category","Features"],["Dengue without warning signs","Fever + 2 of: nausea, rash, aches, tourniquet +, leucopenia"],["With warning signs","Abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, fluid accumulation, mucosal bleed, lethargy, hepatomegaly, ↑ HCT with ↓ platelets"],["Severe dengue","Shock/respiratory distress from leakage, severe bleeding, organ impairment"]]],
["Aedes larval indices",[["Index","Definition"],["House index","% houses positive for larvae/pupae"],["Container index","% water containers positive"],["Breteau index","Positive containers per 100 houses (> 50 high risk)"],["Pupal index","Pupae per 100 houses"]]],
["Control measures",["Source reduction — weekly dry day, cover containers","Temephos, Bti, larvivorous fish","Fogging/ULV during outbreaks","Personal protection (repellents, full sleeves, nets)","Community participation, National Dengue Day 16 May","Sentinel surveillance (NS1, IgM)"]]],
{c:"Dengue, transmitted by day-biting container-breeding Aedes mosquitoes, is controlled mainly by community-based source reduction, larval control, personal protection and early recognition of warning signs with careful fluid management."});

Z("cm4",3,"cm",["HIV/AIDS is caused by the human immunodeficiency virus transmitted sexually, through blood and from mother to child; India's National AIDS Control Programme (NACP Phase V, 2021–26) aims to reduce new infections and AIDS deaths by 80% (from 2010) and achieve 95-95-95 targets through prevention, testing, treatment (Test and Treat with TLD) and elimination of vertical transmission.","HIV control is a chain: prevent infection (condoms, safe blood, PPTCT, targeted interventions), find those infected (testing), treat everyone (ART) so the virus becomes undetectable and untransmittable.",["Epidemiology (India): ~24.6 lakh PLHIV (2021), adult prevalence ~0.21%, new infections ~63,000/year, high in Mizoram, Nagaland, Manipur; key populations — FSW, MSM, IDU, transgender, migrants, truckers; routes — heterosexual (~85%), parent-to-child, IDU, blood (<1%).","Prevention: targeted interventions for key populations, condom promotion, STI management (Suraksha clinics, syndromic kits), blood safety (NAT, voluntary donation), opioid substitution therapy, needle–syringe exchange, PPTCT (lifelong TLD to mother, infant NVP), PEP, PrEP, IEC (Red Ribbon Clubs, 1097 helpline).","Testing and treatment: ICTCs (Integrated Counselling and Testing Centres), community-based screening; 'Test and Treat' — ART for all regardless of CD4 (TLD first-line), viral load monitoring, ART centres, Link ART, Centres of Excellence; care — cotrimoxazole, TB preventive therapy.","NACP history: I (1992), II (1999), III (2007), IV (2012–17, extended to 2021), V (2021–26; 95-95-95 by 2025, eliminate vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis); HIV/AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017 — anti-discrimination, confidentiality."],"95-95-95: 95% know status, 95% of them on ART, 95% of those virally suppressed."],
"HIV/AIDS: acquired immunodeficiency syndrome is the advanced stage of infection with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV-1, predominant in India — subtype C; HIV-2 — West Africa, some in India), a retrovirus that infects CD4+ T lymphocytes, macrophages and dendritic cells, causing progressive immunodeficiency and opportunistic infections. Global: ~39 million PLHIV, 1.3 million new infections and 6.3 lakh deaths (2022). India (HIV Estimations 2021/23, NACO): ~24.6–25 lakh PLHIV (third largest number globally), adult (15–49) prevalence 0.21% (declining from 0.55% in 2000), ~63,000–66,000 new infections annually (48% decline since 2010), ~42,000 AIDS-related deaths (76% decline); highest prevalence in Mizoram (2.7%), Nagaland, Manipur, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka, Maharashtra; concentrated epidemic among key populations — female sex workers (~1.8%), men who have sex with men (~3.3%), people who inject drugs (~9%), hijra/transgender (~3.8%), plus bridge populations (migrants, truckers). Modes of transmission: sexual (heterosexual ~85% in India; homosexual), parent-to-child (in utero, intrapartum, breastfeeding — 30–45% without intervention, < 2% with PPTCT), parenteral (sharing needles among IDUs, transfusion of infected blood — now < 0.1% with screening, unsafe injections, needle-stick injuries — 0.3%), organ transplantation. Factors increasing transmission: high viral load (acute infection, advanced disease), STIs (genital ulcers), multiple partners, unprotected anal sex, lack of male circumcision, cervical ectopy. Natural history: acute HIV syndrome (2–4 weeks), window period (antibody negative — 3–12 weeks; reduced by 4th-generation tests and NAT), asymptomatic phase (median 8–10 years), symptomatic HIV, AIDS (CD4 < 200/µL or AIDS-defining illness — TB most common in India, Pneumocystis pneumonia, cryptococcal meningitis, toxoplasmosis, candidiasis, CMV, Kaposi sarcoma); WHO clinical staging 1–4. Diagnosis (NACO strategy): screening and confirmation by three different rapid tests/ELISA with different antigens (Strategy III for diagnosis in adults), DNA PCR for infants (early infant diagnosis at 6 weeks), viral load for monitoring. National AIDS Control Programme: India's first AIDS case detected in 1986 (Chennai); National AIDS Control Organisation (NACO) established 1992 under MoHFW; State AIDS Control Societies (SACS), District AIDS Prevention and Control Units (DAPCUs); phases — NACP I (1992–99): awareness, blood safety, surveillance; NACP II (1999–2006): targeted interventions, decentralisation to states, free ART from 2004; NACP III (2007–12): halt and reverse the epidemic — scale-up of prevention among high-risk groups, integration with health system, link workers; NACP IV (2012–17, extended to 2021): accelerate reversal, integrate response, Test and Treat policy (2017), viral load testing, 'Mission Sampark' (tracing LFU), HIV/AIDS Act 2017; NACP V (2021–26, fully central sector scheme): goals — 80% reduction in new HIV infections and AIDS-related mortality by 2025–26 from 2010 baseline, elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis, elimination of HIV/AIDS-related stigma and discrimination, universal access to quality STI/RTI services; achieve 95-95-95 targets (95% of PLHIV know their status, 95% of those diagnosed on sustained ART, 95% of those on ART virally suppressed) — aligned with SDG 3.3 (end AIDS as public health threat by 2030). Strategies and components: (1) prevention — (a) targeted interventions (TIs) for key populations through NGOs/CBOs — peer education, condom distribution, STI treatment, behaviour change communication, community mobilisation; harm reduction for PWID — needle–syringe exchange programme, opioid substitution therapy (buprenorphine); (b) link worker scheme and bridge populations (migrants — source and destination, truckers); (c) condom promotion — free (Nirodh), social marketing; (d) STI/RTI control — designated Suraksha clinics, syndromic case management with colour-coded kits, syphilis screening; (e) blood safety — mandatory screening of donated blood for HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis, malaria (NAT in selected centres), promotion of voluntary non-remunerated donation, blood component separation, banning professional donors; (f) prevention of parent-to-child transmission (PPTCT) — universal antenatal HIV and syphilis screening, lifelong ART (TLD) for all pregnant/breastfeeding women with HIV, institutional delivery, infant prophylaxis (nevirapine 6–12 weeks), exclusive breastfeeding, early infant diagnosis; Elimination of Mother-to-Child Transmission (EMTCT) of HIV and syphilis; (g) post-exposure prophylaxis (TLD for 28 days), pre-exposure prophylaxis pilots for key populations; (h) IEC and mainstreaming — mass media, Red Ribbon Clubs in colleges, adolescent education programme, National AIDS helpline 1097, workplace interventions, World AIDS Day (1 December), community-based screening; (2) testing — Integrated Counselling and Testing Centres (ICTCs — standalone, facility-integrated, mobile), community-based screening (CBS), F-ICTC in PHCs, partner notification, index testing; (3) care, support and treatment — free ART through ART centres (> 700), Link ART centres, Centres of Excellence and Paediatric CoEs; Test and Treat (2017); first-line TLD (tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir); routine viral load monitoring; multi-month dispensing; differentiated service delivery; management of opportunistic infections, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis, TB–HIV collaborative activities (intensified case finding, TPT, ART for co-infected), hepatitis co-infection; Care and Support Centres (Vihaan) for adherence, psychosocial support, social protection schemes; (4) strategic information — HIV Sentinel Surveillance (ANC and HRGs), HIV estimations, Integrated Biological and Behavioural Surveillance, SOCH (Strengthening Overall Care for HIV) IT platform; (5) enabling environment — HIV and AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017: prohibits discrimination in employment, education, healthcare, housing, insurance; requires informed consent for testing and confidentiality; ombudsman in states; right to ART; guardianship for minors; stigma reduction; community systems strengthening.",
[["Transmission routes (India)",[["Route","Contribution","Prevention"],["Heterosexual","~85%","Condoms, STI treatment, TIs"],["Parent-to-child","~3–5% (declining)","PPTCT: lifelong ART, infant NVP"],["Injecting drug use","~2%","NSEP, OST"],["Homosexual","~2%","TIs for MSM, condoms, PrEP"],["Blood/products","< 1%","Screening, voluntary donation"]]],
["NACP phases",[["Phase","Years","Focus"],["I","1992–99","Awareness, blood safety, surveillance"],["II","1999–2006","Targeted interventions, free ART (2004)"],["III","2007–12","Halt and reverse; scale-up"],["IV","2012–21","Integration, Test & Treat (2017), HIV Act 2017"],["V","2021–26","80% reduction in infections/deaths; 95-95-95; EMTCT"]]],
["NACP V components",[["Component","Interventions"],["Prevention","TIs, condoms, STI clinics, blood safety, PPTCT, OST, PEP/PrEP, IEC (1097)"],["Testing","ICTCs, community-based screening, index testing"],["Treatment","Test & Treat, TLD, viral load, ART centres, Link ART"],["Care","Cotrimoxazole, TB-HIV, Care & Support Centres"],["Enabling","HIV/AIDS Act 2017, stigma reduction"],["Strategic information","HSS, estimations, SOCH portal"]]]],
{i:"India's HIV epidemic has declined substantially due to targeted prevention and free ART, with NACP V aiming to end AIDS as a public health threat by 2030.",x:[["HIV/AIDS Act 2017",["Anti-discrimination in health, employment, education","Informed consent and confidentiality","Right to treatment (ART)","Ombudsman in each state"]]],f:"Prevention (TIs, condoms, STI, blood safety, PPTCT, OST, PEP/PrEP)\n → Testing (ICTC, CBS) → Linkage → ART (TLD, Test & Treat) → viral suppression (U = U) → ↓ transmission\n Targets: 95-95-95; EMTCT",c:"HIV/AIDS control in India through NACP combines targeted prevention for key populations, blood safety and PPTCT with universal testing and Test-and-Treat ART, supported by the HIV/AIDS Act 2017, to reach 95-95-95 targets and eliminate vertical transmission."});

Z("cm4",4,"cm",["Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium leprae affecting skin and peripheral nerves; India's National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP) aims at zero transmission by 2030 through early case detection (campaigns), free MDT, contact prophylaxis with single-dose rifampicin, disability prevention and rehabilitation, and stigma reduction.","Leprosy is curable with free MDT; the programme hunts for hidden cases, gives a protective pill to contacts, and prevents the disabilities that cause stigma.",["Epidemiology: India has ~53–60% of global new cases (~1 lakh/year); prevalence < 1/10,000 nationally (elimination as public health problem achieved 2005) but pockets (Chhattisgarh, Odisha, Bihar, Jharkhand, Maharashtra); source — untreated MB cases; transmission — droplets/nasal secretions, prolonged close contact; IP 3–5 years (PB) to 9–12 (MB); children affected indicate active transmission.","Classification: PB (1–5 lesions, ≤ 1 nerve, smear −) vs MB (> 5 lesions, > 1 nerve, smear +); Ridley–Jopling (TT, BT, BB, BL, LL), Indian (indeterminate, TT, borderline, LL, pure neuritic).","NLEP (NLCP 1955 → NLEP 1983; MDT): strategies — Leprosy Case Detection Campaign (LCDC), Focused Leprosy Campaign, ABSULS; free uniform MDT (PB 6 months, MB 12 months); SDR-PEP to contacts; Nikusth portal; disability prevention and medical rehabilitation (DPMR) — MCR footwear, self-care, reconstructive surgery (₹12,000 incentive); Sparsh Leprosy Awareness Campaign (30 January); National Strategic Plan 2023–27 — zero transmission by 2030.","Indicators: annual new case detection rate (ANCDR/1 lakh), prevalence rate (/10,000), proportion of grade 2 disability (G2D) among new cases (rate per million — late detection), child proportion, MB proportion, female proportion."],"Leprosy elimination indicator: prevalence < 1/10,000; G2D rate = late detection."],
"Leprosy (Hansen disease) is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium leprae (and M. lepromatosis), primarily affecting skin, peripheral nerves, mucosa of upper respiratory tract and eyes. Burden: globally ~1.8 lakh new cases per year (2022), of which India contributes ~53% (~1 lakh), followed by Brazil and Indonesia; India achieved elimination as a public health problem (prevalence < 1 per 10,000 population) at national level in December 2005, but around 100 districts still exceed this; prevalence ~0.4/10,000 (2023), ANCDR ~7.5/lakh; grade 2 disability ~2% of new cases; child cases ~5% (indicator of ongoing transmission). Epidemiological determinants: agent — obligate intracellular acid-fast bacillus; slowest growing bacterium (generation time 12–14 days), cannot be cultured in vitro (grows in mouse footpad, nine-banded armadillo); predilection for cooler body parts; source — untreated multibacillary (lepromatous) cases discharging bacilli from nasal mucosa (millions per day) and skin; humans are main reservoir (armadillos zoonotic in Americas); infectivity — lepromatous cases most infectious; patients become non-infectious within days of starting rifampicin; transmission — droplet infection (nasal secretions) via respiratory route, prolonged close contact (household contacts at 5–8 times higher risk), possibly skin-to-skin contact and contaminated soil; incubation period — long, average 3–5 years (PB) to 9–12 years (MB), range 6 months to 40 years; host factors — all ages (peak 10–20 years and 30–50), males > females (2:1), genetic susceptibility (HLA, PARK2, NOD2), immune status (cell-mediated immunity determines type — strong CMI → tuberculoid; weak CMI → lepromatous), malnutrition, poverty, overcrowding, poor hygiene; most persons exposed do not develop disease (high natural immunity). Classification: WHO operational (for treatment) — paucibacillary (1–5 skin lesions, no or only one nerve trunk involved, skin smear negative) and multibacillary (6 or more lesions, more than one nerve involved, or any slit-skin smear positive; pure neuritic with > 1 nerve); Ridley–Jopling immunological spectrum — tuberculoid (TT), borderline tuberculoid (BT), mid-borderline (BB), borderline lepromatous (BL), lepromatous (LL), plus indeterminate; Indian classification (1981) — indeterminate, tuberculoid, borderline, lepromatous, pure neuritic. Cardinal signs (case definition): (1) hypopigmented or reddish skin lesion(s) with definite loss/impairment of sensation; (2) involvement of peripheral nerves — thickening with sensory loss; (3) positive skin smear for AFB. Reactions: type 1 (reversal) and type 2 (ENL). Disability grading (WHO): grade 0 — no disability; grade 1 — loss of sensation in hands/feet or eye problems without visible damage; grade 2 — visible damage or deformity (claw hand, foot drop, ulcers, lagophthalmos, visual acuity < 6/60). National Leprosy Eradication Programme: National Leprosy Control Programme (1955; dapsone monotherapy, survey–education–treatment) → National Leprosy Eradication Programme (1983; multidrug therapy introduced in endemic districts) → World Bank–supported NLEP phases (1993–2004; modified leprosy elimination campaigns) → integration with general health services (2002) → elimination at national level (2005) → NLEP under National Health Mission. Current strategy (National Strategic Plan and Roadmap for Leprosy 2023–2027; aligned with WHO Global Leprosy Strategy 2021–2030 'Towards Zero Leprosy'): vision — leprosy-free India; goal — zero transmission (zero new autochthonous cases) by 2030, ahead of SDG; key strategies — (1) early case detection: Leprosy Case Detection Campaign (LCDC; door-to-door search by ASHA and male volunteer teams in high-endemic districts since 2016), Focused Leprosy Campaign (for grade 2 disability cases in a village), special campaigns for hard-to-reach areas, integration with Ayushman Bharat-HWCs and school health (Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram), Ayushman Bharat Sparsh Leprosy Unmulan Abhiyan (ABSULS); (2) prompt treatment: free uniform three-drug MDT (rifampicin, clofazimine, dapsone) for all patients — PB 6 months, MB 12 months — in blister calendar packs through all government health facilities; monitoring of drug resistance (sentinel surveillance) and second-line regimens; (3) prevention: chemoprophylaxis with single-dose rifampicin (SDR-PEP) for contacts of new cases (household, neighbourhood, social), contact screening; Mycobacterium indicus pranii (MIP/Mw) vaccine (immunoprophylaxis/immunotherapy) in some districts; (4) disability prevention and medical rehabilitation (DPMR): early detection and treatment of reactions and neuritis with steroids, self-care practices (soaking, oiling, ulcer care), provision of MCR footwear, splints, aids and appliances, reconstructive surgery in designated centres with incentive to patients (₹12,000) and institutions; (5) stigma and discrimination reduction: Sparsh Leprosy Awareness Campaign (on Anti-Leprosy Day, 30 January — Martyrs' Day), repeal of discriminatory laws, involvement of persons affected; (6) surveillance and information: Nikusth 2.0 web-based reporting portal (patient-wise), case-based surveillance; (7) capacity building and research (ICMR-NJIL&OMD Agra). Indicators: prevalence rate (cases on record per 10,000 population at end of year), annual new case detection rate (ANCDR per 1,00,000), proportion of MB, female, child cases among new cases, grade 2 disability rate per million population and proportion among new cases (target zero G2D in children), treatment completion rate (> 90%), relapse rate, proportion of contacts receiving SDR-PEP.",
[["Classification",[["Feature","Paucibacillary","Multibacillary"],["Skin lesions","1–5","≥ 6"],["Nerves involved","0–1","> 1"],["Skin smear","Negative","Positive (any)"],["Ridley–Jopling","TT, BT (indeterminate)","BB, BL, LL"],["MDT duration","6 months","12 months"]]],
["NLEP strategies",[["Strategy","Activities"],["Early detection","LCDC, Focused Leprosy Campaign, ABSULS, HWC, school health"],["Treatment","Free uniform MDT (R + C + D)"],["Prevention","SDR-PEP to contacts; MIP vaccine"],["DPMR","Reaction management, self-care, MCR footwear, reconstructive surgery (₹12,000)"],["Stigma reduction","Sparsh campaign (30 January)"],["Surveillance","Nikusth 2.0 portal"],["Goal","Zero transmission by 2030 (NSP 2023–27)"]]],
["Programme indicators",[["Indicator","Meaning"],["Prevalence rate","Cases/10,000 (elimination < 1)"],["ANCDR","New cases/1,00,000/year"],["G2D rate/proportion","Late detection"],["Child proportion","Active transmission"],["MB proportion","Infectious pool"],["Treatment completion","Programme quality (> 90%)"]]]],
{i:"India eliminated leprosy as a public health problem in 2005 but still reports over half of global new cases; the focus is now on interrupting transmission.",x:[["WHO disability grades",["Grade 0: no disability","Grade 1: loss of sensation, no visible damage","Grade 2: visible deformity (claw hand, foot drop, ulcer, lagophthalmos)"]]],f:"Untreated MB case (nasal discharge) → droplets → prolonged close contact → infection (long IP 3–12 yr) → PB/MB disease\n NLEP: detect (LCDC) → MDT → SDR-PEP contacts → DPMR → stigma reduction → zero transmission 2030",c:"Leprosy control under NLEP relies on active case detection, free MDT, single-dose rifampicin prophylaxis for contacts, disability prevention and rehabilitation, and stigma reduction, with indicators such as ANCDR and G2D guiding progress towards zero transmission by 2030."});

Z("cm4",5,"cm",["Poliomyelitis, caused by poliovirus (types 1, 2, 3) spread by the faeco-oral route, can cause acute flaccid paralysis; India was certified polio-free in 2014 (last case January 2011) through pulse polio campaigns, routine immunisation with OPV and IPV, and sensitive acute flaccid paralysis (AFP) surveillance.","AFP surveillance is the 'smoke detector' for polio: every child with sudden floppy weakness is tested so that any hidden poliovirus is caught early.",["Epidemiology: enterovirus; 1 paralytic case per ~200 infections (iceberg); faeco-oral; IP 7–14 days; wild type 2 eradicated (2015), type 3 (2019); WPV1 persists in Pakistan, Afghanistan; risk of VDPV (vaccine-derived) and importation.","Eradication strategies: high routine OPV coverage (bOPV), IPV (fractional fIPV at 6, 14 weeks and 9 months), National Immunisation Days (pulse polio — all children < 5 yr), Sub-National Immunisation Days, mop-up rounds, environmental (sewage) surveillance; tOPV → bOPV switch (April 2016).","AFP surveillance: all cases of AFP in children < 15 years (or any age if polio suspected) reported immediately; 2 stool samples 24–48 h apart within 14 days of onset, reverse cold chain, WHO-accredited labs; 60-day follow-up for residual paralysis.","Performance indicators: non-polio AFP rate ≥ 2/1 lakh children < 15 years (India target), adequate stool collection ≥ 80%, case investigation within 48 h ≥ 80%; NPSP/WHO support; certification (WHO SEAR polio-free 27 March 2014)."],"AFP surveillance: '<15 years, 2 stools, 24 h apart, within 14 days, NP-AFP rate ≥ 2'."],
"Poliomyelitis is an acute viral infection caused by poliovirus (enterovirus, Picornaviridae; three serotypes — 1, 2, 3; type 1 most paralytogenic and epidemic), which multiplies in the oropharynx and intestine and may invade the central nervous system destroying anterior horn cells, causing acute flaccid paralysis. Epidemiological features: reservoir — humans only (no animal reservoir, no long-term carriers — basis for eradication); source — faeces and pharyngeal secretions of cases and inapparent infections (most common); infectiousness — virus excreted in faeces for 3–6 weeks; mode of transmission — faeco-oral (main route in developing countries — contaminated water, food, hands, flies) and droplet (early phase); incubation period 7–14 days (range 3–35); host — children < 5 years mainly (infantile paralysis) in endemic areas; males > females for paralysis; precipitating factors — fatigue, trauma, IM injections ('provocation polio'), tonsillectomy, pregnancy; immunity — type-specific; ratio of inapparent to paralytic infections ~200–1000:1 (iceberg phenomenon) — outcomes: inapparent infection (90–95%), abortive polio (minor illness), non-paralytic polio (aseptic meningitis), paralytic polio (< 1% — spinal, bulbar, bulbospinal); environment — poor sanitation, overcrowding, summer–monsoon season. Global Polio Eradication Initiative (1988, WHA resolution): cases declined > 99.9% from ~3,50,000 in 1988; wild poliovirus type 2 declared eradicated (2015), type 3 (2019); wild poliovirus type 1 remains endemic only in Pakistan and Afghanistan; circulating vaccine-derived polioviruses (cVDPV, especially type 2) cause outbreaks in under-immunised populations; novel OPV2 (nOPV2) used for outbreak response. India: last wild poliovirus case — Howrah, West Bengal, 13 January 2011; removed from list of endemic countries in February 2012; WHO South-East Asia Region (including India) certified polio-free on 27 March 2014. Eradication strategies (India Pulse Polio Programme launched 1995–96): (1) high routine immunisation coverage — OPV at birth (zero dose), 6, 10, 14 weeks and booster at 16–24 months; bivalent OPV (types 1 and 3) replaced trivalent OPV on 25 April 2016 (globally synchronised 'switch') to eliminate type 2 VDPV risk; inactivated polio vaccine introduced in 2015 — now fractional intradermal IPV (fIPV, 0.1 mL) at 6 and 14 weeks and third dose at 9 months (2023) to maintain type 2 immunity; (2) supplementary immunisation activities — National Immunisation Days (pulse polio — two doses of OPV to all children < 5 years irrespective of previous vaccination, on a single day at booths followed by house-to-house search for missed children), Sub-National Immunisation Days in high-risk states, mop-up campaigns around any positive case, special strategies for high-risk groups (migrants, nomads, brick kilns, construction sites, transit vaccination at bus/railway stations, border posts), use of monovalent OPV1/3 in Uttar Pradesh and Bihar (2005–10); (3) AFP surveillance — highly sensitive surveillance to detect every case of polio; (4) environmental surveillance — sewage sampling in major cities to detect poliovirus circulation (wild and VDPV) in absence of cases; (5) post-certification — maintaining high coverage, surveillance, outbreak preparedness and response plan, containment of poliovirus in laboratories (GAPIII), vaccination of travellers from endemic countries (OPV certificate requirement for travel from Pakistan, Afghanistan and others). Acute flaccid paralysis surveillance (since 1997, with National Polio Surveillance Project — WHO–Government of India): case definition — any child under 15 years of age with acute onset of flaccid paralysis for which no obvious cause (such as severe trauma) is found, or paralytic illness at any age in which polio is suspected; AFP includes polio, Guillain–Barré syndrome, transverse myelitis, traumatic neuritis, enterovirus 71, etc.; procedures — immediate reporting (zero reporting weekly by all reporting units including private practitioners), case investigation within 48 hours of notification by surveillance medical officer, collection of two adequate stool specimens (8–10 g each) 24–48 hours apart within 14 days of onset of paralysis, transported in reverse cold chain (2–8 °C) to WHO-accredited polio laboratory (8 in India) arriving within 72 hours in good condition; virus isolation and intratypic differentiation (wild, Sabin-like, VDPV); 60-day follow-up examination for residual paralysis; classification — confirmed polio (wild virus isolated), VDPV, polio-compatible (inadequate stools with residual paralysis/death/lost to follow-up — reviewed by expert committee), discarded (non-polio AFP); contacts sampled in some cases. Surveillance quality indicators: non-polio AFP rate ≥ 2 per 1,00,000 children under 15 years per year (WHO minimum 1; India uses ≥ 2 — achieved > 10 in high-risk states), ≥ 80% of AFP cases with adequate stool specimens, ≥ 80% investigated within 48 h of notification, ≥ 80% specimens arriving at lab within 3 days and in good condition, ≥ 80% follow-up examinations at 60 days, completeness and timeliness of reporting ≥ 90%.",
[["Epidemiology",[["Feature","Detail"],["Agent","Poliovirus types 1, 2, 3 (enterovirus)"],["Reservoir","Humans only"],["Transmission","Faeco-oral, droplet"],["Incubation","7–14 days"],["Inapparent:paralytic","~200–1000:1"],["India","Last case 13 Jan 2011; certified 27 Mar 2014"],["Global","WPV2 (2015), WPV3 (2019) eradicated; WPV1 in Pakistan, Afghanistan"]]],
["Eradication strategies",["High routine coverage: bOPV + fIPV","Pulse polio NIDs/SNIDs (< 5 yr)","Mop-up rounds, transit and migrant vaccination","AFP surveillance","Environmental (sewage) surveillance","tOPV → bOPV switch (2016)","Containment and outbreak preparedness"]],
["AFP surveillance indicators",[["Indicator","Target"],["Non-polio AFP rate","≥ 2/1 lakh children < 15 yr"],["Adequate stool collection","≥ 80% (2 samples 24–48 h apart within 14 days)"],["Investigation within 48 h","≥ 80%"],["Samples reaching lab ≤ 3 days, good condition","≥ 80%"],["60-day follow-up","≥ 80%"],["Reporting completeness/timeliness","≥ 90%"]]]],
{c:"India eliminated polio through high-quality pulse polio campaigns, routine OPV/IPV immunisation and sensitive AFP and environmental surveillance; maintaining these until global eradication is essential."});

Z("cm4",6,"cm",["Measles is a highly contagious viral disease (paramyxovirus) spread by respiratory droplets/airborne route, causing fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots and rash; it is a major cause of child deaths through complications, preventable by two doses of measles–rubella (MR) vaccine, with India targeting elimination.","Measles spreads like wildfire (R0 12–18) — almost every unvaccinated child exposed gets it; vaccination with two doses is the firebreak.",["Epidemiology: only human reservoir; infectivity 4 days before to 4 days after rash; secondary attack rate > 90%; IP 10 days (to rash 14 days); one attack — lifelong immunity; malnutrition, vitamin A deficiency → severe disease.","Complications: diarrhoea, pneumonia (most common cause of death), otitis media, encephalitis, SSPE (late), malnutrition, xerophthalmia/blindness, TB flare.","Prevention: live attenuated MR vaccine (Edmonston-Zagreb) 0.5 mL SC at 9–12 months and 16–24 months (UIP); MR campaign (9 months–15 years, 2017–); vaccine within 72 h of exposure protects contacts; immunoglobulin within 6 days for vulnerable.","Management: supportive, vitamin A (2 doses — 50,000/1 lakh/2 lakh IU by age) reduces mortality; isolation 4 days after rash; case-based measles–rubella surveillance (fever + rash); elimination target (India 2026)."],"Measles 3 Cs: Cough, Coryza, Conjunctivitis + Koplik spots."],
"Measles (rubeola) is an acute, highly infectious viral disease caused by an RNA paramyxovirus (Morbillivirus) with only one serotype. Burden: despite vaccine, measles caused ~1.36 lakh deaths globally in 2022, mostly children under 5; India has reduced measles deaths substantially but outbreaks continue in pockets with low coverage. Epidemiological determinants: agent — single serotype; inactivated by heat, light, acid; source — cases only (no carriers, no animal reservoir — eradicable); infective material — secretions of nose, throat and respiratory tract; period of communicability — 4 days before to 4 days after onset of rash (most infectious during prodrome); secondary attack rate among susceptible household contacts > 90%; basic reproductive number R0 12–18 (most contagious human disease) — herd immunity threshold ~95%; host — age: in developing countries 6 months–3 years (maternal antibodies protect for first 6–9 months), shift to older age with immunisation; both sexes; immunity — one attack confers lifelong immunity; nutrition — malnourished children (especially vitamin A deficient) suffer severe disease with high case fatality (up to 10–30% in outbreaks); environment — winter and spring; overcrowding, poor living conditions; mode of transmission — droplet infection and droplet nuclei (airborne), direct contact; portal of entry — respiratory tract and conjunctiva; incubation period — about 10 days from exposure to onset of fever, 14 days to appearance of rash (vaccine virus IP ~7 days — basis for post-exposure vaccination within 72 h). Clinical features: prodromal stage (3–5 days) — fever, coryza, cough, conjunctivitis (3 Cs), photophobia, Koplik spots (bluish-white spots on red buccal mucosa opposite lower molars — pathognomonic, appear 2 days before rash); eruptive stage — maculopapular dusky red rash starting behind ears, spreading to face, trunk and limbs over 3 days; post-measles stage — brownish discolouration, fine desquamation, weight loss, immunosuppression (several weeks). Complications (especially in malnourished, < 5 years, adults, immunocompromised): diarrhoea and dysentery, pneumonia (most common cause of death — primary viral giant cell or secondary bacterial), otitis media, croup, febrile convulsions, encephalitis (1 in 1000), subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE — 7–10 years later), corneal ulceration and keratomalacia (vitamin A deficiency — major cause of childhood blindness), stomatitis, malnutrition (kwashiorkor precipitation), reactivation of tuberculosis (tuberculin anergy), immune amnesia. Prevention and control: (1) active immunisation — live attenuated measles vaccine (Edmonston-Zagreb strain in India) now given as measles–rubella (MR) combined vaccine; freeze-dried, reconstituted with sterile water (diluent kept cold), used within 4 hours; dose 0.5 mL subcutaneously in right upper arm; schedule (UIP) — first dose at 9–12 months (with vitamin A first dose), second dose at 16–24 months; catch-up up to 5 years; seroconversion ~85% at 9 months and > 95% after second dose; immunity long-lasting; contraindications — pregnancy, severe immunodeficiency, recent immunoglobulin; adverse reactions — fever, rash (5–7 days later), rarely febrile seizures, thrombocytopenia, anaphylaxis, toxic shock syndrome (programmatic error — contaminated reconstituted vial); MR vaccination campaign (2017–2019) for children 9 months–15 years covering > 32 crore children; (2) post-exposure prophylaxis — measles vaccine given within 3 days (72 h) of exposure can prevent or modify disease (vaccine virus has shorter incubation); human normal immunoglobulin (0.25 mL/kg IM; 0.5 mL/kg in immunocompromised) within 6 days of exposure for susceptible high-risk contacts (infants < 6 months, pregnant women, immunocompromised); (3) case management — symptomatic and supportive; nutritional support; vitamin A supplementation — two doses 24 h apart (< 6 months 50,000 IU, 6–11 months 1,00,000 IU, ≥ 12 months 2,00,000 IU; third dose after 2–4 weeks if eye signs) — reduces mortality and complications by ~50%; treat complications (antibiotics for pneumonia/otitis, ORS for diarrhoea); (4) isolation — of cases from onset of catarrhal stage until 4 days after rash appears (limited value in community because infectious before diagnosis); exclusion from school; (5) surveillance — case-based measles–rubella surveillance under IDSP/WHO (fever with maculopapular rash) with laboratory confirmation (serum IgM within 28 days of rash; throat swab/urine for genotyping) through accredited measles–rubella laboratories; outbreak investigation and response (outbreak response immunisation); (6) measles elimination goal — India adopted goal of measles and rubella elimination (originally 2020, revised to 2023, now 2026); indicators — incidence < 1 per million, ≥ 95% coverage with two doses in all districts, discarded non-measles non-rubella rate ≥ 2/1 lakh. Measles is a potential candidate for global eradication.",
[["Epidemiology",[["Feature","Detail"],["Agent","Measles virus (paramyxovirus), one serotype"],["Reservoir","Humans only"],["Transmission","Droplets, airborne"],["Infectivity","4 days before to 4 days after rash"],["R0 / SAR","12–18 / > 90%"],["IP","10 days (fever), 14 days (rash)"],["High risk","Malnourished, vitamin A deficient, < 5 yr"]]],
["Complications",["Diarrhoea, pneumonia (leading cause of death)","Otitis media, croup","Encephalitis, SSPE","Xerophthalmia, blindness","Malnutrition, TB reactivation"]],
["Prevention and control",[["Measure","Details"],["MR vaccine","0.5 mL SC; 9–12 and 16–24 months"],["Post-exposure","Vaccine within 72 h; immunoglobulin within 6 days"],["Vitamin A","2 doses (50,000 / 1 lakh / 2 lakh IU by age)"],["Isolation","Until 4 days after rash"],["Surveillance","Case-based fever–rash; IgM confirmation"],["Goal","Measles–rubella elimination (2026)"]]]],
{c:"Measles is extremely contagious and deadly in malnourished children; two doses of MR vaccine, vitamin A therapy, post-exposure vaccination and case-based surveillance underpin India's elimination effort."});

Z("cm4",7,"cm",["Diarrhoeal diseases, including cholera, are spread by the faeco-oral route through contaminated water, food and hands and are a leading cause of child deaths; prevention relies on safe water, sanitation, hygiene, breastfeeding and vaccines, and treatment on ORS and zinc; cholera is controlled by case management, chlorination, surveillance and oral cholera vaccine.","Diarrhoea is the 'F-diagram' disease: faeces → fluids, fields, flies, fingers, food → new host; break the chain with toilets, clean water and handwashing.",["Burden: ~13% under-5 deaths in India historically (now ~5–9%); causes — rotavirus (most common in infants), ETEC, Shigella, Vibrio cholerae, Campylobacter, Giardia, Cryptosporidium.","Cholera: V. cholerae O1 (El Tor, classical) and O139; carriers; IP hours to 5 days; rice-water stools, severe dehydration; reservoir — humans, aquatic environment; outbreaks in monsoon, floods, fairs.","Control: ORS (low osmolarity) + zinc (14 days), continued feeding; antibiotics for cholera (doxycycline single dose/azithromycin), dysentery (ciprofloxacin/azithromycin); STOP Diarrhoea campaign (formerly IDCF), ORS–zinc corners.","Prevention: safe water (chlorination — residual 0.5 mg/L), sanitation (Swachh Bharat, ODF), handwashing with soap (critical times), exclusive breastfeeding, rotavirus vaccine (UIP — 6, 10, 14 weeks), measles vaccine, vitamin A, food hygiene; oral cholera vaccine (Shanchol/Euvichol — 2 doses) in high-risk areas/outbreaks; surveillance (IDSP), outbreak response."],"Diarrhoea treatment: 'ORS + Zinc + Feeding'; prevention: 'WASH + breastfeeding + rotavirus vaccine'."],
"Diarrhoeal diseases: diarrhoea is the passage of three or more loose or liquid stools per day; types — acute watery diarrhoea (including cholera), dysentery (bloody), persistent diarrhoea (≥ 14 days). Burden: diarrhoea remains a leading cause of under-five deaths globally (~4.4 lakh annually) and in India (contributing ~5–9% of child deaths; ~50,000–1 lakh deaths) despite marked decline; also causes malnutrition (vicious cycle), stunting and impaired cognitive development. Agents: viruses (rotavirus — most common cause of severe dehydrating diarrhoea in infants < 2 years; norovirus, adenovirus, astrovirus), bacteria (enterotoxigenic and enteropathogenic E. coli, Shigella, Vibrio cholerae, Campylobacter, Salmonella, Clostridioides difficile), protozoa (Giardia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora), helminths. Reservoir and source: human cases and carriers (faeces); animals for some (Salmonella, Campylobacter). Transmission: faeco-oral — contaminated water ('waterborne'), food (especially weaning foods, street foods), hands (fingers), flies, fomites — illustrated by the 'F-diagram' (faeces → fluids, fields, flies, fingers → food → mouth). Host factors: age (6–24 months — weaning, loss of maternal antibodies), malnutrition, lack of breastfeeding, measles, vitamin A and zinc deficiency, immunodeficiency. Environmental and social factors: unsafe water supply, poor sanitation and open defecation, poor personal and food hygiene, overcrowding, poverty, low maternal education, floods, monsoon season, mass gatherings (melas), migration. Cholera: acute diarrhoeal disease caused by toxigenic Vibrio cholerae serogroups O1 (biotypes classical and El Tor; serotypes Ogawa, Inaba, Hikojima) and O139 (Bengal); El Tor predominant in current seventh pandemic (since 1961); Gram-negative comma-shaped motile bacillus; enterotoxin (cholera toxin) activates adenylate cyclase → cAMP ↑ → massive secretion of water and electrolytes; reservoir — humans (cases, convalescent, contact carriers) and aquatic environment (copepods, brackish water); infective dose high (10⁶–10⁸; lower with achlorhydria); incubation period few hours to 5 days (usually 1–2 days); ratio of cases to carriers ~1:25–100 for El Tor; clinical — sudden painless profuse watery 'rice-water' stools, vomiting, rapid severe dehydration, muscle cramps, shock, acidosis, renal failure; case fatality > 50% untreated, < 1% with proper rehydration; endemic in West Bengal, Odisha, Maharashtra, Gujarat etc.; outbreaks after floods; notifiable under IHR. Control of diarrhoeal diseases: (1) case management — oral rehydration therapy (WHO low-osmolarity ORS 245 mOsm/L), zinc supplementation (20 mg/day × 14 days; 10 mg in < 6 months), continued feeding including breastfeeding, appropriate antibiotics only when indicated (cholera — doxycycline 300 mg single dose adults, azithromycin 20 mg/kg children; bloody diarrhoea — ciprofloxacin/azithromycin; amoebiasis/giardiasis — metronidazole), IV fluids (Ringer lactate) for severe dehydration (Plan C), avoid antidiarrhoeals and antiemetics in children; Diarrhoea Treatment and Training Units; ORS–zinc corners in health facilities; Intensified Diarrhoea Control Fortnight (2014) now STOP Diarrhoea Campaign (June–August annually) — prophylactic ORS distribution by ASHAs to households with under-5 children, zinc, awareness on handwashing; (2) prevention — (a) safe drinking water (piped supply, chlorination — maintain residual free chlorine 0.5 mg/L at consumer end; household water treatment — boiling, chlorine tablets, filters; Jal Jeevan Mission — tap water to every rural household), (b) sanitation — sanitary latrines, elimination of open defecation (Swachh Bharat Mission 2014 — ODF India declared 2019; ODF Plus), safe disposal of child faeces, solid waste management, fly control, (c) hygiene — handwashing with soap at critical times (after defecation, after cleaning child, before preparing food, before feeding/eating) — reduces diarrhoea by ~40%; Global Handwashing Day (15 October), (d) food hygiene — WHO five keys to safer food, safe weaning foods, (e) nutrition — exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months (protects strongly), appropriate complementary feeding, vitamin A supplementation, (f) immunisation — rotavirus vaccine in UIP (Rotavac/Rotasiil at 6, 10, 14 weeks; nationwide since 2019), measles vaccine (reduces post-measles diarrhoea), typhoid conjugate vaccine, oral cholera vaccine (killed whole-cell bivalent O1/O139 — Shanchol, Euvichol; 2 doses 14 days apart; protects ~65% for 3–5 years; used in endemic hotspots and outbreak response; Hillchol — Indian vaccine 2024); (3) cholera control specific — early detection through surveillance (IDSP — clustering of acute watery diarrhoea triggers investigation; stool culture), verification and notification, case management in treatment centres, chemoprophylaxis (not recommended for mass use; selective for household contacts in high-risk settings), disinfection of excreta and soiled linen (hypochlorite), chlorination of wells and water supply, inspection of food establishments, health education, sanitation improvement, oral cholera vaccine, coordination with municipal agencies, international travel measures (IHR notification; vaccination certificate not required); Global Task Force on Cholera Control — 'Ending Cholera: Global Roadmap to 2030'.",
[["Common agents",[["Group","Examples"],["Viruses","Rotavirus (infants), norovirus, adenovirus"],["Bacteria","ETEC, Shigella, V. cholerae, Campylobacter, Salmonella"],["Protozoa","Giardia, E. histolytica, Cryptosporidium"]]],
["Cholera epidemiology",[["Feature","Detail"],["Agent","V. cholerae O1 (El Tor, classical), O139"],["Reservoir","Humans (cases, carriers), aquatic environment"],["IP","Few hours–5 days"],["Clinical","Rice-water stools, severe dehydration"],["Case:carrier (El Tor)","1:25–100"],["CFR","> 50% untreated, < 1% treated"]]],
["Control measures",[["Area","Measures"],["Case management","ORS + zinc + feeding; antibiotics if indicated; IV RL for severe"],["Water","Chlorination (residual 0.5 mg/L), Jal Jeevan Mission"],["Sanitation","Latrines, ODF (Swachh Bharat), fly control"],["Hygiene","Handwashing with soap at critical times"],["Nutrition","Exclusive breastfeeding, vitamin A"],["Vaccines","Rotavirus (UIP), measles, oral cholera vaccine"],["Programme","STOP Diarrhoea campaign, IDSP surveillance"]]]],
{c:"Diarrhoeal diseases and cholera are controlled by breaking faeco-oral transmission through safe water, sanitation and hygiene, protecting children with breastfeeding and rotavirus vaccine, and preventing deaths with ORS, zinc and appropriate antibiotics, supported by surveillance and oral cholera vaccine."});

Z("cm4",8,"cm",["Rabies is an almost invariably fatal viral encephalitis (lyssavirus) transmitted mainly by bites of rabid dogs; it is 100% preventable by prompt post-exposure prophylaxis — wound washing, rabies vaccine and rabies immunoglobulin for category III exposures — and by dog vaccination; India aims for zero dog-mediated human rabies deaths by 2030 (NAPRE).","Rabies is 'certain death if untreated, certain survival if treated properly' — wash the wound immediately, vaccinate, and give immunoglobulin for deep bites.",["Epidemiology: India ~36% of global rabies deaths (~18,000–20,000/year estimated); dogs cause ~96%; others — cats, monkeys, mongoose, jackals, bats (not rodents usually); IP 1–3 months (range days–years; shorter for head/face bites).","WHO categories: I — touching/feeding, licks on intact skin (no PEP); II — nibbling uncovered skin, minor scratches without bleeding (wound care + vaccine); III — single/multiple transdermal bites or scratches, licks on broken skin, mucosal contamination, bat exposure (wound care + vaccine + RIG).","PEP: wash wound with soap and running water for 15 minutes, antiseptic (povidone-iodine); no suturing (delay or loose after RIG); vaccine — IM Essen (days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28) or 4-dose (0, 3, 7, 14–28) or ID updated Thai Red Cross (2-2-2-0-2: days 0, 3, 7, 28); RIG — HRIG 20 IU/kg or ERIG 40 IU/kg infiltrated into wound on day 0 (or rabies mAb).","Pre-exposure prophylaxis: days 0, 7 (and 21/28) for high-risk (vets, lab workers, dog handlers, children in endemic areas); prevention — mass dog vaccination (≥ 70%), animal birth control (ABC rules), responsible pet ownership; NRCP → National Action Plan for Dog Mediated Rabies Elimination (NAPRE) 2030; rabies notifiable."],"PEP: 'Wash 15 min, Vaccinate, Immunoglobulin for Category III'."],
"Rabies (hydrophobia) is an acute, highly fatal viral disease of the central nervous system caused by rabies virus (Lyssavirus, Rhabdoviridae; bullet-shaped RNA virus), primarily a zoonosis of warm-blooded animals transmitted to man by bites or licks of infected animals. Burden: ~59,000 human deaths annually worldwide; India accounts for about 36% (~18,000–20,000 estimated; reported numbers lower), mostly children; ~1.5–2 crore animal bites/year; rabies is endemic throughout India except Andaman & Nicobar and Lakshadweep islands. Epidemiological determinants: agent — rabies virus (street virus from naturally infected animals; fixed virus — passaged in laboratory, used for vaccines); inactivated by sunlight, heat, soap, detergents, alcohol, iodine, quaternary ammonium compounds; reservoir — urban rabies (dogs — 96% of human cases in India; cats), sylvatic rabies (jackals, wolves, foxes, mongooses, bats — in Americas); monkeys, cattle, horses can transmit; rodents (rats, squirrels) and rabbits rarely transmit — PEP generally not required; source — saliva of rabid animal (virus present in saliva 3–5 days before onset of symptoms in dogs and throughout illness; a dog that remains healthy for 10 days after the bite did not transmit rabies at the time — basis for 10-day observation); mode of transmission — animal bites (most common), licks on abraded skin or mucosa, scratches, aerosol (bat caves, laboratories — rare), corneal and organ transplants, human-to-human (theoretically); incubation period — usually 1–3 months (range 4 days to several years); shorter with bites on head, face, neck, hands (richly innervated), multiple and deep bites, children; host — all ages; children 5–15 years most affected (more contact with dogs, bites on face); males > females; occupational groups (veterinarians, dog handlers, lab workers). Natural history and clinical features: virus multiplies locally in muscle, enters peripheral nerves and travels centripetally to CNS by retrograde axoplasmic flow (~3–12 mm/day), replicates in brain (Negri bodies), then spreads centrifugally to salivary glands and other organs; prodrome — fever, malaise, paraesthesia/pain at bite site; acute neurological phase — furious (encephalitic) rabies (80%: hydrophobia — painful spasms of pharyngeal muscles on attempting to drink, aerophobia, hyperexcitability, hallucinations, autonomic instability) or paralytic (dumb) rabies (20%: ascending flaccid paralysis); coma and death within 1–2 weeks; essentially 100% case fatality once symptoms appear. Diagnosis: clinical; confirmation by direct fluorescent antibody test on corneal impression smear/skin biopsy (nuchal), RT-PCR on saliva/CSF, antibody in CSF, post-mortem brain — Negri bodies, DFA (gold standard). Management of animal bites (post-exposure prophylaxis — National Guidelines for Rabies Prophylaxis, NCDC 2019): classification of exposure (WHO): category I — touching or feeding animals, animal licks on intact skin (no exposure) — no PEP (wash exposed area); category II — nibbling of uncovered skin, minor scratches or abrasions without bleeding — immediate wound washing and rabies vaccine; category III — single or multiple transdermal bites or scratches, contamination of mucous membrane or broken skin with saliva from animal licks, exposures due to direct contact with bats — immediate wound washing, rabies immunoglobulin and rabies vaccine. Components of PEP: (1) local wound treatment (immediately, most important) — flush and wash the wound thoroughly with soap/detergent and running water for at least 15 minutes, apply antiseptic (povidone-iodine, alcohol); do not apply irritants (chillies, oil, turmeric); avoid suturing (if unavoidable, delay or loosely suture after infiltrating RIG); tetanus prophylaxis and antibiotics as needed; (2) passive immunisation (category III) — rabies immunoglobulin to neutralise virus at the wound site before vaccine-induced antibodies develop (7–14 days): human rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG) 20 IU/kg or equine rabies immunoglobulin (ERIG, purified F(ab')2) 40 IU/kg, or rabies monoclonal antibodies (Rabishield, Twinrab — 3.33 IU/kg); entire calculated dose (or as much as anatomically feasible) infiltrated into and around all wounds on day 0 (or up to day 7 after first vaccine dose); not given IM at distant site (WHO 2018 — only wound infiltration); not given if previously vaccinated; skin test for ERIG not mandatory; (3) active immunisation — modern cell culture/embryonated egg vaccines (purified chick embryo cell vaccine — PCECV, purified Vero cell vaccine — PVRV, human diploid cell vaccine — HDCV, purified duck embryo vaccine) with potency ≥ 2.5 IU per IM dose; nerve tissue vaccines discontinued (2004); regimens — intramuscular: Essen 5-dose regimen (1 mL/0.5 mL IM in deltoid — never gluteal — on days 0, 3, 7, 14 and 28), or 4-dose Essen (0, 3, 7, 14–28) or Zagreb (2-1-1: two doses day 0, one each on days 7 and 21); intradermal (cost-saving, approved in India since 2006): updated Thai Red Cross regimen — 0.1 mL ID at two sites on days 0, 3, 7 and 28 (2-2-2-0-2); WHO 2018 one-week ID regimen (2 sites on days 0, 3, 7); PEP must be completed irrespective of delay since bite (no time limit — start even months later); pregnancy and infancy are not contraindications; immunocompromised — full IM regimen with RIG for category II and III; re-exposure in previously fully vaccinated persons — local wound care plus booster vaccine on days 0 and 3 (no RIG); observation of biting dog/cat for 10 days (if animal remains healthy, PEP may be modified to pre-exposure regimen — not applicable in WHO's newer guidance to stop; in India, continue full course). Pre-exposure prophylaxis: for high-risk groups — veterinarians, animal handlers, laboratory staff, dog catchers, wildlife workers, travellers to endemic areas, children in highly endemic areas; regimen — IM or ID on days 0 and 7 (WHO 2018; India — days 0, 7, 21 or 28); booster on re-exposure. Prevention and control in animals: mass dog vaccination (vaccinating ≥ 70% of dog population annually interrupts transmission — key to elimination), animal birth control (sterilisation) programmes (Animal Birth Control Rules 2023), registration and vaccination of pet dogs, elimination of stray dogs not permitted (Supreme Court; ABC instead), garbage management, quarantine of imported animals, community awareness. Programme: National Rabies Control Programme (12th Five Year Plan) → National Action Plan for Dog Mediated Rabies Elimination from India by 2030 (NAPRE, launched 2021 by MoHFW and Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying — One Health approach); components — availability of PEP (vaccine and RIG free in all government facilities, model anti-rabies clinics), capacity building, IEC, surveillance (rabies made a notifiable disease, IHIP reporting of animal bites and rabies cases), laboratory network, mass dog vaccination; World Rabies Day 28 September (Louis Pasteur's death anniversary).",
[["WHO categories of exposure",[["Category","Type of exposure","Management"],["I","Touching/feeding, licks on intact skin","Wash; no PEP"],["II","Nibbling uncovered skin, minor scratches without bleeding","Wound care + vaccine"],["III","Transdermal bites/scratches, licks on broken skin/mucosa, bat contact","Wound care + RIG + vaccine"]]],
["Rabies vaccine regimens",[["Regimen","Route","Schedule"],["Essen (5-dose)","IM deltoid","0, 3, 7, 14, 28"],["Essen (4-dose)","IM","0, 3, 7, 14–28"],["Zagreb (2-1-1)","IM","0 (×2), 7, 21"],["Updated Thai Red Cross","ID 0.1 mL × 2 sites","0, 3, 7, 28"],["Pre-exposure","IM/ID","0, 7 (21/28)"],["Re-exposure (vaccinated)","IM/ID","0, 3 (no RIG)"]]],
["Rabies immunoglobulin",[["Type","Dose"],["HRIG","20 IU/kg"],["ERIG (purified)","40 IU/kg"],["Rabies mAb","3.33 IU/kg"],["Administration","Infiltrate into and around all wounds on day 0 (up to day 7)"]]],
["Control measures",["Prompt wound washing (15 min soap and water)","Free PEP in government facilities","Mass dog vaccination (≥ 70%)","Animal birth control","Notification, surveillance (IHIP)","NAPRE 2030 (One Health)","World Rabies Day 28 September"]]],
{i:"Rabies kills thousands in India every year although it is entirely preventable with timely PEP and dog vaccination.",x:[["Wound management do's and don'ts",["Do: wash with soap and running water 15 min; povidone-iodine","Don't: apply chillies, oil, turmeric; suture immediately","Tetanus prophylaxis, antibiotics if needed"]]],f:"Rabid dog bite → virus in muscle → peripheral nerve (retrograde) → CNS (IP 1–3 months) → encephalitis → salivary glands → death\n PEP: wound washing + RIG (Cat III, local) + vaccine (Essen/ID) → neutralisation before CNS entry",c:"Rabies, nearly always fatal once symptomatic, is fully preventable by immediate wound washing, timely vaccination and wound infiltration with RIG for category III exposures, while mass dog vaccination under NAPRE aims to eliminate dog-mediated rabies by 2030."});

Z("cm4",9,"cm",["Leptospirosis, scrub typhus and plague are important zoonoses in India: leptospirosis is spread by water contaminated with urine of infected rats (floods, paddy fields), scrub typhus by bites of infected trombiculid mite larvae (chiggers), and plague by fleas of infected rodents; all are treated with doxycycline or other antibiotics and prevented by rodent/vector control and protective measures.","These diseases come to humans from animals: rats' urine in floodwater (leptospirosis), mites in scrubland vegetation (scrub typhus), and rat fleas (plague).",["Leptospirosis: Leptospira interrogans; reservoir — rats, cattle, dogs; transmission — contact of abraded skin/mucosa with water/soil contaminated by urine (monsoon floods, farmers, sewer workers); IP 7–12 days; Weil's disease (jaundice, renal failure, haemorrhage), pulmonary haemorrhage; treatment — doxycycline (mild), penicillin/ceftriaxone (severe); prophylaxis — doxycycline 200 mg weekly in high-risk exposures; endemic — Kerala, Gujarat, Maharashtra, TN, Karnataka, Andamans.","Scrub typhus: Orientia tsutsugamushi; vector and reservoir — larval trombiculid mite (chigger, Leptotrombidium deliense); rodents; IP 6–21 days; eschar (black necrotic), fever, lymphadenopathy, pneumonitis, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis, AES; diagnosis — IgM ELISA, Weil–Felix OX-K; treatment — doxycycline (or azithromycin in pregnancy/children); prevention — protective clothing, repellents, clearing vegetation.","Plague: Yersinia pestis; reservoir — wild rodents; vector — rat flea Xenopsylla cheopis (blocked flea); forms — bubonic, septicaemic, pneumonic (person-to-person droplets); India — Surat 1994 (pneumonic), Himachal 2002; treatment — streptomycin/gentamicin, doxycycline; chemoprophylaxis — doxycycline/tetracycline for contacts; control — flea control first (insecticides), then rodent control; surveillance; Cheopis index > 1 = danger.","General zoonosis control: One Health approach, IDSP surveillance, rodent and vector control, health education, protective equipment."],"Plague control: kill fleas first, then rats (else fleas move to humans)."],
"Zoonoses are diseases and infections naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and man; India has a high burden owing to close human–animal contact. Leptospirosis: a re-emerging spirochaetal zoonosis caused by pathogenic Leptospira interrogans (> 250 serovars — icterohaemorrhagiae, canicola, pomona, autumnalis). Reservoir — rodents (rats — most important; lifelong renal carriage and urinary shedding), cattle, buffaloes, pigs, dogs, goats; transmission — indirect contact of skin abrasions, cuts or mucous membranes (conjunctiva, oral, nasal) with water, moist soil or vegetation contaminated with urine of infected animals (most common), direct contact with urine, blood or tissues of infected animals; occupational groups — farmers (paddy and sugarcane fields), sewage workers, veterinarians, butchers, dairy workers, fishermen, miners, soldiers; seasonal — outbreaks during and after monsoon, floods and waterlogging (Kerala floods 2018, Mumbai); endemic regions — Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andaman & Nicobar Islands; incubation period 7–12 days (2–30); clinical — biphasic illness; anicteric leptospirosis (90% — fever, myalgia especially calves, headache, conjunctival suffusion); severe icteric leptospirosis (Weil's disease — jaundice, acute kidney injury, haemorrhage), leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (high mortality), myocarditis, aseptic meningitis, uveitis; diagnosis — IgM ELISA, rapid tests, microscopic agglutination test (MAT — reference), PCR, dark-field microscopy, culture (EMJH medium); treatment — mild: doxycycline 100 mg BD × 7 days or amoxicillin/azithromycin; severe: IV penicillin G, ceftriaxone, cefotaxime; supportive (dialysis, ventilation); prevention — avoid wading in floodwater, protective clothing (boots, gloves), wound covering, rodent control, vaccination of animals, sanitation, health education, chemoprophylaxis with doxycycline 200 mg once weekly for high-risk exposure (flood relief workers, farmers in endemic areas during season); Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis (PPCL, NCDC) in endemic states — surveillance, laboratory strengthening, case management, IEC, intersectoral coordination. Scrub typhus (tsutsugamushi disease): rickettsial zoonosis caused by Orientia tsutsugamushi; widespread in 'tsutsugamushi triangle' including India (Himachal, Uttarakhand, J&K, Northeast, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Karnataka, Odisha, Rajasthan); important cause of acute undifferentiated fever and acute encephalitis syndrome (Gorakhpur AES outbreaks); reservoir and vector — larval stage (chigger) of trombiculid mites (Leptotrombidium deliense, L. akamushi) — maintained by transovarial transmission; rodents are hosts; humans are accidental hosts infected when chiggers feed on them in 'mite islands' (scrub vegetation, grassland, forest clearings, riverbanks, rice fields, gardens); season — rainy and post-monsoon months; occupational — farmers, labourers, soldiers, campers; incubation period 6–21 days (~10 days); clinical — fever, headache, myalgia, eschar (painless black crusted necrotic lesion at bite site — axilla, groin, waist, inframammary — seen in 40–80%), regional lymphadenopathy, maculopapular rash, conjunctival suffusion, hepatosplenomegaly; complications — pneumonitis/ARDS, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis, acute kidney injury, shock, multiorgan dysfunction (mortality 6–30% untreated); diagnosis — IgM ELISA (preferred), indirect immunofluorescence, PCR (eschar), Weil–Felix test (OX-K agglutination — low sensitivity); treatment — doxycycline 100 mg BD × 7 days (drug of choice; dramatic response within 48 h — therapeutic diagnostic), azithromycin (pregnancy, children), chloramphenicol, rifampicin (resistant areas); IV doxycycline/azithromycin for severe; empirical doxycycline in AES/AUF in endemic areas; prevention — personal protection (long clothing, repellents — DEET, permethrin-impregnated clothing), clearing vegetation around houses (mite control), avoid sitting/lying on grass, chemoprophylaxis (doxycycline weekly) for high-risk groups; no vaccine. Plague: an acute infectious zoonosis of rodents caused by Yersinia pestis (Gram-negative, bipolar-staining coccobacillus), transmitted to humans by bites of infected rodent fleas; historically caused pandemics (Black Death); India — Surat outbreak 1994 (pneumonic and bubonic, Beed district Maharashtra), Himachal Pradesh 2002 (pneumonic, Hatkoti), Uttarakhand 2004; natural foci persist in Deccan plateau (Karnataka–Andhra–Maharashtra border), Himachal–Uttarakhand, Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris); reservoir — wild rodents (sylvatic/enzootic plague — field rodents Tatera indica, the Indian gerbil — most important in India) and domestic rodents (Rattus rattus — urban/domestic plague); vector — rat flea Xenopsylla cheopis (most efficient; flea becomes 'blocked' when plague bacilli multiply in its proventriculus, causing it to regurgitate bacilli while feeding), X. astia, X. brasiliensis; transmission — flea bite (bubonic), contact with infected tissues (hunters), inhalation of droplets from pneumonic cases (person-to-person — pneumonic plague is highly contagious); incubation period — bubonic 2–7 days, pneumonic 1–3 days; clinical forms — bubonic (painful swollen lymph node — bubo — in groin, axilla, neck; fever; CFR 50–60% untreated), septicaemic (primary or secondary; DIC, gangrene — 'Black Death'), pneumonic (secondary or primary; severe pneumonia with haemoptysis; nearly 100% fatal if untreated within 24 h); diagnosis — Gram/Wayson stain of bubo aspirate, sputum, blood (safety-pin appearance), culture, F1 antigen RDT, PCR, serology (passive haemagglutination); treatment — streptomycin (drug of choice) or gentamicin; doxycycline, ciprofloxacin, chloramphenicol (meningitis); isolation (strict respiratory isolation for pneumonic plague for 48 h after starting treatment); control measures — (1) early diagnosis and treatment of cases, notification (IHR — immediately notifiable), strict isolation of pneumonic cases; (2) chemoprophylaxis for contacts — tetracycline 500 mg QID or doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days, cotrimoxazole for children and pregnant women; (3) vector control — insecticidal dusting (DDT, malathion, carbaryl, deltamethrin) of rat burrows, runways and houses to kill fleas must precede rodent control (otherwise fleas leave dead rats and bite humans); (4) rodent control — rodenticides (zinc phosphide, warfarin, bromadiolone), rat-proofing of buildings, sanitation, garbage disposal; (5) surveillance — rodent surveillance (rat fall, serology), flea surveillance — flea indices: general flea index (average number of fleas per rat), specific flea index (Xenopsylla cheopis index — average number of X. cheopis per rat; > 1.0 indicates potential danger of outbreak), burrow index; human case surveillance (IDSP); plague surveillance units at NCDC Bengaluru; (6) vaccination (killed vaccine — limited use for high-risk lab workers); (7) personal protection (repellents, protective clothing, masks for pneumonic plague); (8) health education.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Leptospirosis","Scrub typhus","Plague"],["Agent","Leptospira interrogans","Orientia tsutsugamushi","Yersinia pestis"],["Reservoir","Rats, cattle, dogs","Trombiculid mites (transovarial), rodents","Wild rodents (Tatera), rats"],["Transmission","Contact with urine-contaminated water/soil","Bite of larval mite (chigger)","Bite of rat flea (X. cheopis); droplets (pneumonic)"],["IP","7–12 days","6–21 days","Bubonic 2–7; pneumonic 1–3 days"],["Key features","Fever, calf pain, conjunctival suffusion, Weil's disease, pulmonary haemorrhage","Eschar, lymphadenopathy, pneumonitis, AES","Bubo, septicaemia, pneumonia"],["Diagnosis","IgM ELISA, MAT","IgM ELISA, Weil–Felix OX-K","Bubo aspirate smear/culture, F1 RDT"],["Treatment","Doxycycline; penicillin/ceftriaxone (severe)","Doxycycline; azithromycin","Streptomycin/gentamicin, doxycycline"],["Prophylaxis","Doxycycline 200 mg weekly","Doxycycline weekly","Doxycycline/tetracycline 7 days for contacts"],["Control","Avoid floodwater, protective gear, rodent control","Protective clothing, repellents, vegetation clearing","Flea control first, then rodent control; isolation"]]],
["Plague surveillance indices",[["Index","Meaning"],["General flea index","Fleas per rat"],["Specific (Cheopis) flea index","X. cheopis per rat (> 1 = danger)"],["Burrow index","Burrows per unit area"],["Rat fall","Unusual rat deaths — warning sign"]]]],
{c:"Leptospirosis, scrub typhus and plague are rodent- and vector-linked zoonoses that respond to doxycycline-based treatment; control depends on avoiding exposures, chemoprophylaxis in high-risk settings, rodent and vector control (fleas before rats in plague) and surveillance within a One Health framework."});

Z("cm4",10,"cm",["Japanese encephalitis (JE) is a mosquito-borne flavivirus disease (Culex tritaeniorhynchus) with pigs as amplifying hosts and ardeid birds as reservoirs, causing acute encephalitis in children; lymphatic filariasis is caused by Wuchereria bancrofti and Brugia malayi transmitted by Culex and Mansonia mosquitoes; both are controlled under NCVBDC — JE by vaccination and vector control, filariasis by mass drug administration and morbidity management.","JE spreads pig → mosquito → child (human is a dead-end host); filariasis spreads human → mosquito → human and is eliminated by giving the whole community annual tablets.",["JE: vector Culex vishnui group (C. tritaeniorhynchus) breeding in rice fields; amplifier — pigs; reservoir — herons, egrets; humans dead-end; endemic — UP (Gorakhpur), Bihar, Assam, WB, TN, Karnataka; IP 5–15 days; ~1 in 250 infections symptomatic; CFR 20–30%; survivors neurological sequelae.","JE control: live attenuated SA 14-14-2 / inactivated JENVAC vaccine in UIP (9 months and 16–24 months in endemic districts; adult campaigns); vector control (IRS, fogging, larvivorous fish), pig management (relocation away from homes), AES/JE surveillance, case management, rehabilitation; NPPCJA (2014) multisectoral programme.","Filariasis: W. bancrofti (> 99%, Culex quinquefasciatus — urban, dirty water), B. malayi (Mansonia — Kerala, aquatic plants); nocturnal periodicity (10 pm–2 am night blood smear); clinical — lymphoedema, hydrocele, elephantiasis, tropical pulmonary eosinophilia; diagnosis — night blood smear, filarial antigen test (FTS).","ELF programme (target 2027/2030): MDA annually — DEC + albendazole (DA) or IDA (ivermectin + DEC + albendazole) in high-burden districts; coverage ≥ 65%; transmission assessment survey (TAS); morbidity management and disability prevention (MMDP — limb hygiene, hydrocele surgery); vector control."],"JE: 'Pig amplifies, Culex transmits, child gets encephalitis'; Filaria: 'MDA (DEC + albendazole ± ivermectin)'."],
"Japanese encephalitis (JE) is a mosquito-borne zoonotic viral encephalitis caused by JE virus (flavivirus, closely related to West Nile virus). Burden: leading cause of viral encephalitis in Asia (~68,000 cases, 13,600–20,400 deaths globally each year); in India, endemic in ~300 districts of 24 states — highest burden in Uttar Pradesh (Gorakhpur division), Bihar, Assam, West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, Haryana; outbreaks mainly during monsoon and post-monsoon (in north) — a component of acute encephalitis syndrome (AES), which also includes scrub typhus, enteroviruses, herpes, etc. Epidemiological determinants: agent — JE virus (genotypes I and III in India); natural cycle — virus maintained in nature in pig–mosquito–ardeid bird cycle; reservoir — ardeid water birds (herons, egrets, pond herons); amplifying host — pigs (develop high viraemia without disease; essential for epidemics; pig-rearing near human dwellings increases risk); horses and humans are dead-end hosts (low viraemia — no person-to-person transmission); vectors — Culex vishnui subgroup, mainly Culex tritaeniorhynchus (breeds in rice paddy fields, irrigation channels, ponds; zoophilic, night-biting, outdoor), C. vishnui, C. pseudovishnui, C. gelidus, Mansonia, Anopheles subpictus; extrinsic incubation period 9–12 days; host — children < 15 years most affected in endemic areas (adults in new areas, e.g., Assam); ratio of inapparent to apparent infection ~250–1000:1; incubation period 5–15 days; environment — rice cultivation with irrigation, pig rearing, monsoon temperature and humidity, rural areas; clinical — prodrome of fever, headache, vomiting; acute encephalitic stage — altered sensorium, convulsions, focal deficits, parkinsonian features, coma; case fatality 20–30%; 30–50% of survivors have long-term neuropsychiatric sequelae (cognitive impairment, paralysis, seizures, behavioural problems); diagnosis — IgM capture ELISA in CSF/serum (sentinel sites), RT-PCR. Prevention and control (NCVBDC; National Programme for Prevention and Control of JE/AES — NPPCJA 2014, multisectoral involving health, water, sanitation, women and child development, social justice, education): (1) vaccination — most effective measure: live attenuated SA 14-14-2 vaccine (Chinese) and indigenous inactivated JENVAC (Vero cell, Bharat Biotech) and JEEV; introduced through mass campaigns for children 1–15 years (2006 onwards) followed by routine immunisation in endemic districts — two doses at 9–12 months (with MR) and 16–24 months; adult JE vaccination (15–65 years) in high-burden districts of Assam, UP, WB; (2) vector control — indoor residual spraying in pig sties and human dwellings during transmission season, space spraying/fogging during outbreaks (limited efficacy against exophilic Culex), larviciding, larvivorous fish in paddy fields, intermittent irrigation (alternate wetting and drying of rice fields), environmental management; personal protection — insecticide-treated nets (LLINs), repellents, protective clothing, screening; (3) pig management — relocation of piggeries away (> 4–5 km) from human dwellings, keeping pigs in screened sties, pig vaccination (not widely used); (4) surveillance — AES/JE sentinel surveillance sites and Apex Referral Laboratories, IDSP reporting, entomological and pig serosurveillance; (5) case management — early referral, supportive care in district hospitals and paediatric ICUs (no antiviral treatment); empirical doxycycline/azithromycin for scrub typhus in AES; (6) rehabilitation — physical medicine and rehabilitation departments for disabled survivors; (7) safe drinking water, sanitation and nutrition (AES due to other causes; litchi-associated hypoglycaemic encephalopathy in Muzaffarpur — prevented by ensuring evening meals in children); (8) IEC and community mobilisation. Lymphatic filariasis: a parasitic disease caused by thread-like nematodes — Wuchereria bancrofti (> 99% of cases in India; periodic nocturnal), Brugia malayi (Kerala, coastal and some states; associated with aquatic plants Pistia) and B. timori; adult worms live in lymphatics causing lymphatic damage. Burden: India has ~40% of global LF burden; endemic in ~340 districts in 20 states/UTs (Bihar, UP, Jharkhand, Odisha, Andhra, Telangana, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, WB); ~3 lakh hydrocele and ~6 lakh lymphoedema cases estimated. Epidemiological determinants: reservoir — humans with microfilaraemia (animal reservoir for B. malayi — subperiodic forms in cats, monkeys); vectors — Culex quinquefasciatus (W. bancrofti; breeds in polluted water — drains, cesspools, septic tanks, soakage pits; urban; night biting), Mansonia annulifera/uniformis (B. malayi; larvae attach to roots of aquatic plants like Pistia stratiotes, water hyacinth), Aedes and Anopheles (some regions); extrinsic incubation period 10–14 days; nocturnal periodicity of microfilariae (peak 10 pm–2 am — coinciding with vector biting) — night blood smear examination; incubation period (biological — infection to microfilaraemia) 8–16 months; host — all ages; males more clinical disease; environment — urbanisation, poor drainage and sanitation, open drains, aquatic vegetation (Brugia). Clinical spectrum: asymptomatic microfilaraemia (most), acute adenolymphangitis (ADL attacks), chronic — lymphoedema and elephantiasis of limbs, genitals and breasts, hydrocele (most common chronic manifestation in men), chyluria; tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (occult filariasis). Diagnosis: night blood smear (thick film), membrane filtration, DEC provocation test (daytime), filarial antigen detection (immunochromatographic card test/Filariasis Test Strip — detects W. bancrofti circulating antigen any time), antibody tests (Brugia Rapid), ultrasonography ('filarial dance sign'), PCR. Elimination of Lymphatic Filariasis (ELF) programme (National Filaria Control Programme 1955 → NVBDCP; WHO Global Programme to Eliminate LF 2000; India target revised to 2027 — ahead of SDG 2030): twin pillars — (1) interruption of transmission by mass drug administration (MDA) — annual single-dose treatment of the entire at-risk population (excluding children < 2 years, pregnant women, seriously ill) for at least 5 rounds with effective coverage ≥ 65% of total population: DA regimen — diethylcarbamazine citrate (6 mg/kg; 100 mg tablets by age — 2–5 years 100 mg, 6–14 years 200 mg, ≥ 15 years 300 mg) + albendazole 400 mg (under directly observed administration — drugs swallowed in front of drug administrator); IDA regimen (since 2018 in high-endemic districts) — ivermectin (by height) + DEC + albendazole — achieves elimination faster (single round can clear microfilariae for a year); post-MDA transmission assessment surveys (TAS — school-based antigen testing among 6–7 year children) to decide stopping MDA and post-MDA surveillance; mop-up rounds; 'Sarva Dawa Sevan' and 'Bhagidari' campaigns (2023–24); (2) alleviation of suffering — morbidity management and disability prevention (MMDP): home-based lymphoedema management (washing affected limb with soap and water, drying, skin care, antifungal/antibiotic creams, exercises, elevation, proper footwear) through MMDP kits and camps, treatment of acute attacks, hydrocelectomy surgery free in government facilities, disability certification (lymphatic filariasis included under RPwD Act 2016); supported by vector control (integrated vector management — larvicides like temephos, polystyrene beads in pits, environmental management — drainage, removal of Pistia; personal protection — nets, repellents) and IEC; National Filaria Day (11 November); LF MDA Day.",
[["JE epidemiology",[["Feature","Detail"],["Agent","JE virus (flavivirus)"],["Reservoir","Ardeid birds (herons, egrets)"],["Amplifier","Pigs"],["Vector","Culex tritaeniorhynchus (rice fields)"],["Human","Dead-end host; children < 15 yr"],["Inapparent:apparent","250–1000:1"],["CFR / sequelae","20–30% / 30–50% survivors"]]],
["JE control",[["Measure","Details"],["Vaccination","SA 14-14-2 / JENVAC: 9–12 & 16–24 months (endemic); adult campaigns"],["Vector control","IRS, fogging (outbreaks), larvivorous fish, intermittent irrigation"],["Pig management","Relocate piggeries away from homes"],["Personal protection","LLINs, repellents"],["Surveillance","AES/JE sentinel sites, IgM ELISA"],["Case management & rehab","PICU care, physiotherapy"],["Programme","NPPCJA 2014 (multisectoral)"]]],
["Filariasis",[["Feature","W. bancrofti","B. malayi"],["Distribution","Most of India (> 99%)","Kerala, coastal pockets"],["Vector","Culex quinquefasciatus (dirty water)","Mansonia (aquatic plants — Pistia)"],["Periodicity","Nocturnal","Nocturnal/subperiodic"],["Clinical","Hydrocele, lymphoedema","Lymphoedema (below knee)"]]],
["ELF strategy",[["Pillar","Interventions"],["Interrupt transmission","Annual MDA — DA (DEC + albendazole) or IDA (+ ivermectin); coverage ≥ 65%; TAS"],["Morbidity management","Limb hygiene (MMDP kits), hydrocele surgery, ADL treatment"],["Support","Vector control, IEC, National Filaria Day (11 Nov)"],["Target","Elimination by 2027"]]]],
{i:"JE and filariasis are major vector-borne diseases in India; JE is controlled mainly by vaccination, and filariasis is being eliminated through mass drug administration and morbidity care.",f:"JE: ardeid birds ↔ Culex ↔ pigs (amplify) → Culex → human (dead end) → encephalitis\n Filaria: human microfilaraemia → Culex/Mansonia (EIP 10–14 d) → human → lymphatic damage\n MDA (DA/IDA) → ↓ microfilariae → interrupt transmission",c:"Japanese encephalitis, amplified in pigs and transmitted by rice-field Culex, is prevented chiefly by routine and campaign vaccination plus vector and pig management, while lymphatic filariasis is being eliminated through annual MDA with DEC, albendazole and ivermectin, transmission assessment surveys and morbidity management."});

Z("cm4",11,"cm",["COVID-19, caused by SARS-CoV-2, spreads mainly through respiratory droplets and aerosols; its control relied on testing, isolation, contact tracing, quarantine, non-pharmaceutical interventions (masks, distancing, hand hygiene, ventilation), clinical management and mass vaccination — India administered over 220 crore doses.","COVID control was a layered 'Swiss cheese' model — each layer (masks, distancing, testing, vaccination) has holes, but together they block transmission.",["Epidemiology: SARS-CoV-2 (betacoronavirus); WHO pandemic 11 March 2020 (PHEIC ended May 2023); India — first case 30 January 2020 (Kerala); waves — 2020 (ancestral), 2021 (Delta — severe), 2022 (Omicron — mild, high transmissibility); IP 2–14 days (Omicron ~3 days); R0 2–3 (Omicron higher); CFR ~1.2% overall in India.","Transmission: droplets, aerosols (indoor, poorly ventilated), fomites (minor), pre-symptomatic/asymptomatic spread; high risk — elderly, comorbidities (diabetes, hypertension, obesity, CKD), immunocompromised.","Public health measures: test (RT-PCR, rapid antigen) – track (contact tracing, Aarogya Setu) – treat; isolation and quarantine (home/facility), containment zones, lockdown (March 2020), masks, physical distancing, hand hygiene, ventilation, travel restrictions, surveillance (IDSP/IHIP, INSACOG genomic surveillance).","Vaccination (from 16 January 2021): Covishield (ChAdOx1), Covaxin (inactivated), Corbevax, ZyCoV-D, Covovax, mRNA (Gennova), intranasal iNCOVACC; CoWIN platform; priority groups → all adults → children 12+; precaution doses; >220 crore doses; lessons — health system preparedness, One Health, digital health."],"COVID control: 'Test – Track – Treat – Vaccinate + COVID-appropriate behaviour'."],
"COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) is caused by severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), an enveloped positive-sense RNA betacoronavirus that binds ACE2 receptors via its spike protein; first identified in Wuhan, China (December 2019); probable zoonotic origin (bats; intermediate host uncertain). Epidemiology: WHO declared PHEIC on 30 January 2020 and pandemic on 11 March 2020; PHEIC ended 5 May 2023; > 77 crore reported cases and ~70 lakh reported deaths globally (excess mortality estimates much higher, ~15–20 million); India — first case 30 January 2020 (Thrissur, Kerala); ~4.5 crore cases and ~5.3 lakh reported deaths; three major waves — first (September 2020 peak; ancestral strain), second (April–May 2021; Delta variant B.1.617.2 — severe disease, oxygen shortages, high mortality), third (January 2022; Omicron B.1.1.529 — highly transmissible, immune escape, milder in vaccinated); variants of concern — Alpha, Beta, Gamma, Delta, Omicron and sublineages (BA.2, XBB, JN.1). Epidemiological features: reservoir — humans (animal spillover/spillback — mink, deer); source — infected persons including pre-symptomatic (infectious 1–3 days before symptoms) and asymptomatic individuals; period of infectivity — 2 days before to ~10 days after symptom onset (longer in severe/immunocompromised); transmission — respiratory droplets (close contact), aerosols (airborne — especially indoors, crowded, poorly ventilated settings; superspreading events), contact with contaminated surfaces (minor), vertical (rare); incubation period 2–14 days (median 5 days; Omicron ~3 days); basic reproduction number R0 — ancestral 2–3, Delta 5–7, Omicron higher; secondary attack rate in households 10–30%; host — all ages; severe disease and death more in elderly (> 60), males, those with comorbidities (diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, chronic lung and kidney disease, obesity, cancer, immunosuppression), unvaccinated; children usually mild (MIS-C rare); clinical spectrum — asymptomatic, mild (fever, cough, sore throat, anosmia, fatigue), moderate (pneumonia, SpO2 90–93%), severe (SpO2 < 90%, respiratory rate > 30, ARDS), critical (respiratory failure, shock, multiorgan dysfunction); complications — thrombosis, myocarditis, secondary infections (COVID-associated mucormycosis — 2021 India epidemic linked to uncontrolled diabetes and steroids), long COVID (post-COVID condition); case fatality varied (~1–2% in India overall; infection fatality much lower). Diagnosis: RT-PCR of nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal swab (gold standard), rapid antigen tests (point-of-care, quick), TrueNat/CBNAAT, CT chest, serology (past infection); genomic sequencing (INSACOG — Indian SARS-CoV-2 Genomics Consortium) for variant surveillance. Public health response (India): (1) surveillance — IDSP/IHIP, ILI and SARI surveillance, testing strategy expanded through ICMR laboratory network (> 3000 labs), contact tracing, Aarogya Setu app, genomic surveillance, sero-surveys (ICMR national serosurveys — 67% seroprevalence by July 2021), wastewater surveillance; (2) containment — 'test, track, treat' strategy, case isolation (home isolation for mild; COVID care centres, dedicated COVID health centres and hospitals — three-tier facilities), quarantine of contacts (14 days initially, later 7 days/none), cluster containment and buffer zones, micro-containment zones, nationwide lockdown (25 March 2020; phased unlocking), travel restrictions and screening at points of entry, Vande Bharat Mission; (3) non-pharmaceutical interventions/COVID-appropriate behaviour — face masks (cloth, surgical, N95), physical distancing ('do gaj doori'), hand hygiene, respiratory etiquette, ventilation, avoiding crowds; work from home, closure of schools and mass gatherings; (4) health system strengthening — oxygen supply (PSA plants), ICU beds, ventilators, PPE manufacturing, telemedicine (e-Sanjeevani), health workforce augmentation, Emergency Response and Health Systems Preparedness Package; (5) clinical management (ICMR/AIIMS/MoHFW guidelines) — symptomatic for mild; oxygen, awake proning, corticosteroids (dexamethasone) for moderate/severe; anticoagulants; remdesivir and tocilizumab in selected; avoidance of irrational drugs (HCQ, ivermectin, favipiravir, plasma); (6) vaccination — world's largest vaccination drive launched 16 January 2021: Covishield (Oxford–AstraZeneca ChAdOx1 nCoV-19, Serum Institute), Covaxin (inactivated whole-virion, Bharat Biotech–ICMR), Sputnik V, Corbevax (protein subunit, Biological E), ZyCoV-D (first DNA vaccine), Covovax (Novavax), GEMCOVAC (mRNA), iNCOVACC (intranasal); phased prioritisation — health care workers, frontline workers, ≥ 60 years and 45+ with comorbidities, all ≥ 18, children 15–17 and 12–14, precaution (booster) doses; CoWIN digital platform for registration, scheduling and certificates; > 220 crore doses administered; Vaccine Maitri — export to other countries; AEFI surveillance (rare TTS with vector vaccines, myocarditis with mRNA); (7) risk communication — IEC, helplines (1075), combating infodemic; (8) social protection — PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana (free food grains), insurance for health workers. Lessons learnt: importance of preparedness, surveillance and laboratory capacity, genomic surveillance, digital health, public health workforce, equity (migrant workers' crisis), One Health approach, local manufacturing of vaccines and medical supplies, clear communication, evidence-based therapy, and the Pandemic Accord/IHR amendments.",
[["Epidemiological features",[["Feature","Detail"],["Agent","SARS-CoV-2 (betacoronavirus)"],["Transmission","Droplets, aerosols, (fomites)"],["Infectious period","2 days before to ~10 days after onset"],["IP","2–14 days (median 5; Omicron ~3)"],["R0","2–3 (ancestral) → higher (Delta, Omicron)"],["High risk","Elderly, comorbidities, unvaccinated"],["India waves","2020 ancestral, 2021 Delta, 2022 Omicron"]]],
["Control measures",[["Area","Measures"],["Surveillance","Testing (RT-PCR, RAT), contact tracing, INSACOG, serosurveys"],["Containment","Isolation, quarantine, containment zones, lockdown"],["NPIs","Masks, distancing, hand hygiene, ventilation"],["Health system","Three-tier facilities, oxygen, ICU, telemedicine"],["Treatment","Oxygen, dexamethasone, anticoagulation; avoid irrational drugs"],["Vaccination","Covishield, Covaxin, Corbevax, ZyCoV-D, mRNA; CoWIN; > 220 crore doses"],["Communication","IEC, helpline 1075, infodemic management"]]],
["Lessons learnt",["Preparedness and surge capacity","Genomic and wastewater surveillance","Digital health platforms","Equity and social protection","One Health approach","Evidence-based treatment"]]],
{i:"COVID-19 demonstrated the need for integrated surveillance, health system resilience and rapid vaccination in pandemic response.",f:"Infected person (incl. pre-/asymptomatic) → droplets/aerosols → susceptible (ACE2) → infection (IP ~5 d) → mild/severe\n Control: test-track-isolate-quarantine + masks, distancing, ventilation + vaccination → ↓ transmission, ↓ severe disease",c:"COVID-19 epidemiology — respiratory and aerosol transmission with pre-symptomatic spread — required layered control through surveillance, isolation and quarantine, non-pharmaceutical interventions, clinical care and the world's largest vaccination drive, offering durable lessons for pandemic preparedness."});
